<node id="TINTAGEL">
  <data key="d0">TINTAGEL</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A location in Cornwall, England. Tintagel is mentioned in the text as a destination during the journey. It is a historic site associated with the legend of King Arthur. The text references Tintagel as part of the journey, indicating its cultural and historical significance.&lt;SEP&gt;Tintagel is a coastal village in Cornwall, England, known for its historic castle and association with the legend of King Arthur. It is described as a wild and dramatic location with a rocky coastline and caves. The area is significant in Arthurian mythology and is mentioned as a place where the narrator took a sail. Its rugged terrain and natural beauty are central to the narrative.&lt;SEP&gt;Tintagel is a historic site located on the coast, known for its ancient ruins and association with legends of King Arthur. It is described as a natural-artificial fortress that has been a landmark between legend and actual history. The site includes a bastion, gateway, and a chapel, and it is believed to have been a significant location in Cornish history. The ruins are worn by wind and waves, and they remain a mysterious and evocative place.&lt;SEP&gt;Tintagel is a location in Cornwall, England, historically associated with King Arthur. It is described as a 'hoar ruin' and is linked to the Arthurian legends, symbolizing the remnants of a bygone era. Its significance lies in its historical and mythical associations, making it an important landmark in the narrative.&lt;SEP&gt;Tintagel is a location in Cornwall, England, known for its historical and mythological associations, particularly with King Arthur. It is described as a place of solitude and beauty, with a church dating back to Saxon times. The location is significant for its connection to Arthurian legend and its scenic beauty.&lt;SEP&gt;Tintagel is a location in Cornwall, England, known for its historical significance as the birthplace of King Arthur. It is mentioned as a destination for the travelers on the thirteenth day of their journey. This location is significant as it represents a legendary and historical site, adding depth to the narrative and the cultural context of the journey.&lt;SEP&gt;Tintagel is an ancient castle in Cornwall, referenced in the text as a location where Queen Guinevere rode through before the dark days of her sin and King Arthur's death. It is historically significant as a site tied to the legend of King Arthur. Tintagel is also connected to the story of Uther Pendragon and Ygrayne, and it plays a key role in the mythos surrounding Arthur's birth.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-68ddd89fc902a4696967a79c5aea8055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d113b2b3dce6fe310bc06ea08b364cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6196f7575ff952a3f5db2b2d296af03f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dca5f3a8de43d3e2bc3bc88b3703f62</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 6}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 31}]</data>
</node>
<node id="OLD HEN">
  <data key="d0">OLD HEN</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">The 'old hen' is a person who leads a group of travelers on a journey, emphasizing caution, order, and comfort. She is described as having a 'vague fear' that her 'chickens might turn out to be ducklings,' indicating she is in a position of authority or guidance. Her role is to ensure the safety and enjoyment of the journey for her companions. She is also the one who sets the rules for the trip, showing her leadership and responsibility.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae</data>
</node>
<node id="JACK THE GIANTKILLER">
  <data key="d0">JACK THE GIANTKILLER</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Jack the Giantkiller is a mythical figure from Cornish folklore, known for his bravery and strength. He is mentioned as an example of a legendary Cornish hero, symbolizing the values of justice and courage. His story has been passed down through generations and is part of the cultural heritage of Cornwall.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 6}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 31}]</data>
</node>
<node id="KING ARTHUR">
  <data key="d0">KING ARTHUR</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">King Arthur is a central figure in the legend, known for founding the Order of Knights of the Round Table. He is portrayed as a legendary king who ruled Britain and sought to uphold justice and virtue. His story includes his birth, rise to power, marriage to Guinevere, and eventual downfall. Arthur is a symbol of chivalry and idealism in medieval literature.&lt;SEP&gt;King Arthur is a legendary British hero and the subject of numerous myths and stories. In this text, he is referred to as the protagonist of a legend that describes a race of people with intense emotions and moral virtues. His grave is mentioned as one of several possible resting places, highlighting the ambiguity and mystery surrounding his true fate. He symbolizes ideals of honor, loyalty, and justice.&lt;SEP&gt;King Arthur is a legendary figure from British mythology, referenced in the text through Tennyson's depiction of him. He is described as an ideal knight, and the text speculates about whether the type of man he represents still exists in modern Cornwall. His presence in the narrative adds a layer of myth and cultural significance.&lt;SEP&gt;King Arthur is a legendary figure from Cornish mythology, known for his leadership and dedication to justice. He is compared to Jack the Giantkiller, highlighting the shared values of courage, self-denial, and the pursuit of right over wrong. His legend continues to influence the cultural identity of Cornwall.&lt;SEP&gt;King Arthur is a legendary figure in British history and mythology, often associated with the Arthurian legends. He is believed to have a spiritual presence in the area described, particularly at Tintagel. His significance lies in his role as a symbol of leadership and heroism. There is no definitive historical evidence of his existence, but he remains a central figure in Cornish folklore and cultural identity.&lt;SEP&gt;King Arthur is a legendary figure referenced in the text, associated with Tintagel and the surrounding ruins. He is described as a 'stainless king' and is linked to the mythic past of the region. His presence is felt through the stories and the ruins, which are believed to be remnants of his reign.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df082bbea6d90a549cd8b56e6131733a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dca5f3a8de43d3e2bc3bc88b3703f62</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 6}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 31}]</data>
</node>
<node id="CORNWALL">
  <data key="d0">CORNWALL</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A coastal region in England known for its rugged landscapes and natural beauty. The text highlights the dangers of the sea and the need for caution when exploring the area. It is a place where nature and human activity coexist, with both risks and rewards.&lt;SEP&gt;A location in Cornwall, known for its ruins of disused tin mines. These mines are described as once thriving but now abandoned, reflecting the decline of the mining industry in the region.&lt;SEP&gt;A location in England known for its natural beauty and historical significance. It is mentioned as the place where the 'honest man' lives and where the narrator and others visit. The region is associated with legends and cultural traditions, such as the story of the Cornwall man-demon, Tregeagle, and the ceremonial cutting of the Loe Pool. Its importance lies in its connection to local folklore and the setting of the narrative.&lt;SEP&gt;A region in England known for its rugged coastline and historical significance in maritime activities. The text highlights the dangers of the coast, including shipwrecks and the presence of coast-guard men.&lt;SEP&gt;Cornwall is a coastal region in England known for its rugged landscapes and rich cultural history. It is described as a place where the spirit of justice and bravery still lingers among the people. The region is associated with the legends of Jack the Giantkiller and King Arthur, and it is the destination of the narrator's journey.&lt;SEP&gt;Cornwall is a county in England, central to the narrative. It is described as having a unique beauty and is the setting for the journey. The region is characterized by its rugged landscapes, granite quarries, and natural features like the Lizard and Trelowarren. Cornwall is essential to the context of the story.&lt;SEP&gt;Cornwall is a county in southwest England, serving as the primary setting for the narrative. It is described as home to a&lt;SEP&gt;Cornwall is a county in the southwest of England, known for its rugged coastlines, historic sites, and cultural heritage. The text describes a journey through this region, emphasizing its natural beauty and historical significance. It is central to the narrative, serving as both a physical and symbolic backdrop for the story of King Arthur and the reflections on the past.&lt;SEP&gt;Cornwall is a region in England where the events of the narrative take place. It is described as having a climate characterized by frequent rain and storms, which significantly influences the mood and activities of the characters. The region is central to the setting and the experiences of the people involved.&lt;SEP&gt;Cornwall is a region in southwest England, known for its rich folklore and historical significance. It is the setting for many of the Arthurian legends and is described as a place where the Cornish people still believe in ghostly stories and supernatural phenomena. The region's cultural and historical context is essential to understanding the narrative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2d3c8ddcaba7527f7fcbf3bec3621635&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dca5f3a8de43d3e2bc3bc88b3703f62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e785b114eb81de96943779a460bab162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-436157d1f1611fd4639ef4c3e3e77cf8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9baf49d70e5cdf906cbc1334a05b60e0</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 18}]</data>
</node>
<node id="EXETER">
  <data key="d0">EXETER</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Exeter is a town in England where the travelers meet before beginning their journey. It is described as a place with a cathedral and a quaint old town, which they visit during their stay. The town serves as a starting point for the journey to Cornwall.</data>
