<node id="PLYMOUTH">
  <data key="d0">PLYMOUTH</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Plymouth is a city in England located near the coast. It is mentioned as a stop along the journey, and it is associated with the Saltash Bridge, which is described as a safe and beautiful structure. The city is part of the route taken by the travelers.&lt;SEP&gt;Plymouth is a coastal city in Devon, England, known for its maritime history and significance as a port. It is mentioned as one of the starting points of the journey. The city has historical importance and is often associated with the Mayflower's departure. Its role in the narrative is as a location where the journey begins.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f15d171fef48d074f3c095117c010dc</data>
</node>
<node id="SALTASH">
  <data key="d0">SALTASH</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Saltash is a town in England located across the River Tamar from Plymouth. It is known for its bridge, which is described as a placid and safe structure. The bridge is compared to the Tay Bridge, which was destroyed in a disaster, highlighting the contrast between the two structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Saltash is a town located in Cornwall, England, situated on the River Tamar. It is noted for its bridge, which is compared to the Tay Bridge. The bridge is described as spanning placidly over the river, symbolizing safety and beauty. Saltash plays a key role in the narrative as a location that the travelers admire.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f15d171fef48d074f3c095117c010dc</data>
</node>
<node id="LAND'S END">
  <data key="d0">LAND'S END</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A coastal area known for its dramatic cliffs and scenic views. It is a significant landmark in the region and is mentioned as a place of historical interest. The location is described as having picturesque rocks and a beautiful natural setting. It is associated with legends and historical figures, such as Tristram and Iseult. The Land's End is also a place where tourists gather, though it is noted that the local community is relatively isolated from tourism.&lt;SEP&gt;A geographical feature at the westernmost point of England. It is described as a dramatic landscape with ocean views on both sides. The location is central to the characters' journey and is considered a destination of great interest.&lt;SEP&gt;A location in Cornwall, England, known for its dramatic coastline and natural beauty. It is mentioned as the place where the sun sets into the sea, creating a golden mist. This location is central to the narrative and symbolizes the beauty of nature.&lt;SEP&gt;A location known for its scenic beauty and historical significance. It is mentioned as a place that the group visited during their travels, and it is described as a place with a 'ghostly' or 'might have been' feel. The Land's End is central to the group's experience and emotions.&lt;SEP&gt;A place in Cornwall known for its natural beauty and historical significance. It is the destination of the travelers and is associated with several events and stories, making it a key location in the narrative.&lt;SEP&gt;Land's End is a headland in Cornwall, England, known for its dramatic coastal scenery. It is mentioned as a place that the characters seldom go near. This location is significant as it represents a remote and less-visited part of the landscape, contrasting with the more populated areas like Marazion. It is associated with the natural beauty of the region and serves as a point of reference in the narrative.&lt;SEP&gt;Land's End is a headland in Cornwall, known for its dramatic coastline and scenic beauty. It is the final destination of the journey, and the narrator expresses a deep personal desire to see it. The location is described as a favorite landscape-dream from childhood, symbolizing the narrator's longing for adventure and discovery.&lt;SEP&gt;Land's End is a rocky headland in Cornwall, England, known for its dramatic coastal scenery and historical significance. It is described as the farthest point where footing is possible, offering a view of the vast Atlantic Ocean. This location is central to the narrative, symbolizing solitude and the connection between land and sea. It is associated with the natural beauty and spiritual reflection mentioned in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;The Land's End is a prominent headland in Cornwall, marking the westernmost point of the English mainland. It is described as stretching into the horizon, where sea and sky meet. This location is significant for its symbolic and geographical importance, representing the edge of the land and the meeting point of nature and imagination.&lt;SEP&gt;The Land's End is the westernmost point of the island, marked by a public house called the First and Last Inn. It is a symbolic endpoint of the journey, representing the edge of the world. The location is described as remote and isolated, with limited tourist activity on Sundays.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a5e39286406a99236327034e4840f578&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d113b2b3dce6fe310bc06ea08b364cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4223cdc7e9877a27944679ddd0c14739&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9294464d408e527bddad8101fcc42d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fb3571cbea81efe6dfda2aa2199fdbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df082bbea6d90a549cd8b56e6131733a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd2b50b24a25833ce136611502f99de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca4427317d91b282aad105e4c1fcb34</data>
