<node id="SEPTEMBER 1ST, 1881">
  <data key="d0">SEPTEMBER 1ST, 1881</data>
  <data key="d1">DATE</data>
  <data key="d2">September 1st, 1881, is the date when the journey begins. It is described as a lovely day, and the start of the trip is seen as a positive omen. This date marks the beginning of the travelers' adventure and is significant in the narrative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae</data>
</node>
<node id="DESTRUCTION OF THE TAY BRIDGE">
  <data key="d0">DESTRUCTION OF THE TAY BRIDGE</data>
  <data key="d1">EVENT</data>
  <data key="d2">The destruction of the Tay Bridge is an event that is referenced in the text. It is described as a tragic incident that occurred in a previous year, where a bridge collapsed due to a storm, resulting in loss of life. This event is used as a contrast to the more stable and safe Saltash Bridge, highlighting the dangers of nature and the importance of caution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae</data>
</node>
<node id="GERRANS">
  <data key="d0">GERRANS</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Gerrans is a small fishing village in Cornwall, England. It is mentioned as a quiet place recommended by an old gentleman. The village is described as being almost as quiet as a grave, and it is suggested as a potential destination for the travelers seeking peace and solitude.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4f15d171fef48d074f3c095117c010dc</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 9}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 40}]</data>
</node>
<node id="OLD GENTLEMAN">
  <data key="d0">OLD GENTLEMAN</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who is described as an elderly gentleman with a thoughtful and wise demeanor. He is portrayed as someone who values the independence of his daughters and shares similar views with the narrator about the importance of caution and wisdom. His role in the narrative is to provide guidance and support to the narrator.&lt;SEP&gt;The old gentleman is a character in the narrative who offers advice to the travelers. He is described as having a west-country accent and a comfortable stoutness, suggesting he may be from Devon. His role is to provide guidance and warnings about the Cornish people, influencing the travelers' decisions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4f15d171fef48d074f3c095117c010dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c527f463c6b54a8cb919a64d12423b24</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 9}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 40}]</data>
</node>
<node id="GREEN BANK HOTEL">
  <data key="d0">GREEN BANK HOTEL</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">The Green Bank Hotel is a lodging establishment in Falmouth, England. It is contrasted with the more modern Falmouth Hotel, and it is chosen by the travelers for its homely peace and comfort. The hotel is significant as a location where the travelers decide to stay instead of the more luxurious option.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4f15d171fef48d074f3c095117c010dc</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 9}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 40}]</data>
</node>
<node id="TAY BRIDGE">
  <data key="d0">TAY BRIDGE</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">The Tay Bridge is a notable structure in Scotland, mentioned as a reference point for the Saltash bridge. It is described as a 'wonderful work of engineering art' that was destroyed by a blast. The bridge serves as a comparison to the Saltash bridge, highlighting the latter's safety and beauty.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4f15d171fef48d074f3c095117c010dc</data>
</node>
<node id="WE">
  <data key="d0">WE</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who is part of a group of travelers. They are described as being in a humble room and enjoying the natural beauty of the Cornish coast. Their role is to experience and reflect on the surroundings, highlighting the contrast between nature and art.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who is part of the group observing the luminous circle around the sun. They are described as being keenly interested in the Lizard Lights but unable to fully understand the technology involved. Their role is to witness and reflect on the experience.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who is part of the narrative, described as 'we' in the text. They are traveling and experiencing the journey, making decisions about accommodations and transportation. Their role is central to the story, as they are the ones navigating through different locations and interacting with other characters. They are significant because their experiences and choices drive the narrative forward. They are not explicitly named but are referred to as 'we.'</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b0c1bb6db9932af72e242802e01101&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3bdb332242dccc51989976c4f0ce2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12e1d0ddbd52f7c9b5db9fe78dbfee83</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 41}]</data>
</node>
<node id="FRIEND">
  <data key="d0">FRIEND</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A friend who meets the travelers in Falmouth and helps them with practical arrangements, such as finding lodging and sending telegrams. She is described as a 'good genius of practicality,' indicating her helpful and resourceful nature. Her role is crucial in facilitating the travelers' journey, and she is remembered fondly for her assistance. She is not explicitly named.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b0c1bb6db9932af72e242802e01101</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 41}]</data>
</node>
<node id="LIZARD">
  <data key="d0">LIZARD</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A location in Cornwall known for its lighthouse, which guides ships through dark nights. The Lizard Lights are described as bright and visible from far distances, serving as a critical navigational aid.&lt;SEP&gt;A location in Cornwall known for its long-lived residents. It is referenced in the context of the register books that record the ages of individuals, including one who lived to be 120 years old. This location is significant for its historical and demographic characteristics, and it is associated with the rector and the community's social activities.&lt;SEP&gt;A location in Cornwall where the storm took place. It is described as being particularly severe, with strong winds and heavy rain. The Lizard storm is noted as being more awful than other storms the narrator has experienced. This location is significant due to the intense weather conditions it endured. It is directly related to the storm and the experiences of the narrator. The Lizard is known for its unique weather patterns, which are highlighted in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;A location in Cornwall, England, known for its scenic beauty and as a destination for sightseers. It is mentioned as being almost better than Land's End. The Lizard is significant as it is the next destination for the travelers, and it plays a key role in the narrative. Its description includes its natural beauty and the fact that it is a place of interest for tourists.&lt;SEP&gt;A location known for its Lizard Lights, which are described as an impressive technological marvel. It is a place where the group decides to investigate despite not fully understanding the technology behind it. The Lizard is also associated with dangerous coastal conditions and shipwrecks.&lt;SEP&gt;A location that serves as a key point of interest in the narrative. It is described as a high plain with a sparkling sea beyond it, and it is home to the Lizard Lights, a large low building with a tower at either side. The Lizard is an important landmark in the Cornish coast and is mentioned as a place of exploration.&lt;SEP&gt;Lizard is a location in Cornwall where the narrative takes place. It is mentioned as a place with heavy rain and a post office, which serves as an important point of commerce. The Lizard is central to the events described, including the arrival of Charles and the general atmosphere of the town.&lt;SEP&gt;Lizard is a location that the group visits. It is described as a place that initially seemed like a mere word or dot on a map but later becomes meaningful to the group. The Lizard is significant as it is the setting of the narrative and holds personal and emotional value for the characters.&lt;SEP&gt;The Lizard is a headland in Cornwall, known for its scenic beauty and historical significance. It is a key location in the narrative where the characters spend time exploring and enjoying the surroundings. The Lizard is described as a place of peace and tranquility, with notable features such as the Lizard Lights and the surrounding natural landscape. It plays a central role in the story as the primary destination of the journey.&lt;SEP&gt;The Lizard is a headland in Cornwall, mentioned as a nearby location to the village where the narrator stayed. It is described as having a different climate compared to the village, indicating its geographical significance. The Lizard is also referenced in relation to the journey taken by the narrator and Charles, suggesting it is a notable landmark in the region.&lt;SEP&gt;The Lizard is a headland in Cornwall, referenced as a destination in the journey. It is described as a place of natural beauty and is associated with the coastal landscape. The Lizard is significant in the context of the narrative as it represents a notable geographical feature of the region.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94e0080d8e548382ec3fd09c122e9b19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cff43d4c0b87d4d234e1ded07f8597b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d3c8ddcaba7527f7fcbf3bec3621635&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3bdb332242dccc51989976c4f0ce2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85b0c1bb6db9932af72e242802e01101&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e785b114eb81de96943779a460bab162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a11868bbedb00db5ad94f79da20acdd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9baf49d70e5cdf906cbc1334a05b60e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e302b5e22b09ab16666a0e39faaadeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfb65c6e4369170c3277959468b587be</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 34}]</data>
</node>
<node id="GREEN BANK">
  <data key="d0">GREEN BANK</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A location in Cornwall, England, where the travelers initially stay before moving on to the Lizard. It is described as an old-fashioned place with a pleasant sea view and bay view. Although it lacks green banks, it is noted for its comfort and homely peace. The Green Bank is significant as it serves as the starting point of the journey and is contrasted with the more modern Falmouth Hotel.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b0c1bb6db9932af72e242802e01101</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 41}]</data>
</node>
<node id="CHARLES">
  <data key="d0">CHARLES</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A person named Charles who acts as a guide or driver for the group. He is described as kind, helpful, and willing to take a longer route to show the travelers more of the area. His role is crucial in facilitating their journey.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who accompanies the narrator and provides guidance. Charles is described as a faithful companion who explains the history of the mining business in Cornwall and helps with navigation.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who drives a carriage. He is described as being considerate of his passengers, ensuring they have a peaceful journey. His actions reflect a professional responsibility towards his passengers.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who drives the travelers' carriage during their journey. He is described as a 'bright-looking, intelligent young fellow' and is given the name Charles by the narrator. His role is important as he ensures the safe and efficient transport of the travelers. He is also noted for his reliability and kindness, which contribute to the overall positive experience of the journey.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who is guiding the narrator through Cornwall. He is described as patient and knowledgeable about the area. He is also concerned with ensuring that the group sees as much of the region as possible. His role is to provide direction and assistance during the journey.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who is part of the group and is described as faithful and helpful. He provides information about the local conditions and helps facilitate the group's activities. His role is essential in ensuring the safety and success of the outing.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who is part of the group exploring the Lizard area. He is described as being in the bow of the boat and attending to the 'ladies.' He is also referred to as a 'Knight of the Round Table in humble life,' indicating a sense of chivalry and service.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who is responsible for driving the carriage and is described as 'faithful.' He is reluctant to part from the group and offers to take them on a final drive. His presence and actions influence the group's decisions and provide a sense of comfort.&lt;SEP&gt;Charles is a character in the narrative who is familiar with the local geography and history, though not necessarily with the deeper historical context of places like Goonhilly Down. He serves as a guide and companion to the narrator.&lt;SEP&gt;Charles is a character in the narrative who serves as a guide or companion to the narrator. He is described as being responsible for transporting the narrator and others to different locations. His role is significant as he facilitates travel and provides information about local places and people. He is also shown to have a close relationship with Mary Mundy, the owner of the Old Inn. Charles is portrayed as reliable and helpful, especially in arranging accommodations and ensuring the comfort of the narrator.&lt;SEP&gt;Charles is a character in the narrative, serving as a driver and caretaker for the group's horse. He is described as kind and reliable, ensuring the group's safe travel. His role is essential to the group's movement and comfort during the journey.&lt;SEP&gt;Charles is a character in the text, described as a driver who interacts with the narrator and other passengers. He shares his experiences and emotions, particularly regarding his personal loss. Charles is significant in the context of the narrative as he provides insight into the emotional and personal aspects of the journey.&lt;SEP&gt;Charles is a character who appears in the narrative, bringing a party to the Lizard. He is described as soaked but smiling, showing a positive and caring attitude towards the ladies he is visiting. His presence adds a sense of warmth and connection during the rainy day. He is associated with the Lizard and the events taking place there.&lt;SEP&gt;Charles is a character who helps the narrator navigate the cave. He is described as faithful and considerate, offering assistance and encouragement. His role is crucial in ensuring the narrator's safe passage through the cave and his presence adds a supportive dynamic to the narrative.&lt;SEP&gt;Charles is a character who plays a central role in the decision-making process regarding the trip. He is described as thoughtful and willing to adjust plans to accommodate the group's desires. His importance lies in his ability to influence the group's itinerary and provide practical solutions to logistical challenges. Charles is also involved in the relationship with John Curgenven, as he suggests an alternative plan for visiting the sea-caves.&lt;SEP&gt;Charles is a character who provides assistance to the ladies during their journey. He is described as a faithful and kind individual who ensures their safety and comfort. His role includes driving them along the path and offering guidance. He is also shown to care for his horse, indicating a strong sense of responsibility and dedication. Charles is likely a professional driver or a servant, given his role in helping the ladies with their travel.&lt;SEP&gt;Charles is a person mentioned in the text who accompanies the narrator on the journey. He is described as someone who gives advice and helps navigate the trip. His role is practical and supportive, guiding the group through the landscape.&lt;SEP&gt;Charles is a thoughtful character who offers to prepare the carriage for the travelers. He is described as someone who is always considerate and provides directions to the farm-yard. His actions show a sense of responsibility and care towards the ladies.&lt;SEP&gt;Charles is the landlady's driver who accompanies the travelers. He is described as faithful and loyal, and his role is to transport the travelers to their destinations. This entity is significant as he provides transportation and maintains a connection with the travelers throughout their journey.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94e0080d8e548382ec3fd09c122e9b19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c13d92c83041a8623279efdde8af1707&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-000278539400b09a416764f3bc60efe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ee024234b415f45ea0006087720bb09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cff43d4c0b87d4d234e1ded07f8597b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d3c8ddcaba7527f7fcbf3bec3621635&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad3c1e9f9f896847e83e5929f169df65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85b0c1bb6db9932af72e242802e01101&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-436157d1f1611fd4639ef4c3e3e77cf8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59fd7b8af627bbbd404acb5ac88aef98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d113b2b3dce6fe310bc06ea08b364cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3755e64526057a08de86199d3391703&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9baf49d70e5cdf906cbc1334a05b60e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca4427317d91b282aad105e4c1fcb34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e785b114eb81de96943779a460bab162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fb3571cbea81efe6dfda2aa2199fdbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd2b50b24a25833ce136611502f99de5</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 11}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 45}]</data>
</node>
<node id="CONSTANTINE">
  <data key="d0">CONSTANTINE</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A location in Cornwall, England, mentioned as a village near Falmouth. It is described as having great quarries of granite and is part of the landscape visible from a high ground. Constantine is significant as it is one of the places the travelers visit during their journey and is noted for its natural features.&lt;SEP&gt;Constantine is another village referenced in the text, also noted for its granite quarries. Like Mabe, it contributes to the geographical context of the journey. The presence of these quarries highlights the industrial activity in the region and adds to the scenic description of the area.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b0c1bb6db9932af72e242802e01101&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f</data>
</node>
<node id="MABE">
  <data key="d0">MABE</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Mabe is a village mentioned in the text, known for its granite quarries. It is part of the landscape described in the narrative. Mabe plays a role in the setting of the journey and contributes to the description of the region's geography. The village is associated with the granite industry, which is an important feature of the area.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f</data>
</node>
<node id="DEVON">
  <data key="d0">DEVON</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Devon is a county in England, mentioned in the text as being similar to Cornwall. It is used to contrast the landscape of the region described in the narrative. Devon is significant in the context of the journey and the comparison made by the narrator.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f</data>
</node>
<node id="TRELOWARREN">
  <data key="d0">TRELOWARREN</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Trelowarren is a woodland area in Cornwall, mentioned as part of the journey. It is described as a beautiful and historic site, associated with the Vyvyan family. Trelowarren is significant in the context of the narrative as it represents a natural and cultural landmark in the region.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f</data>
</node>
<node id="HELSTONE">
  <data key="d0">HELSTONE</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A location in Cornwall, England. It is mentioned as a place where the group wishes to stay for an hour or two. It is associated with a "friend" who is part of their network in Cornwall.&lt;SEP&gt;A location in Cornwall, referenced as a place where the narrator and others might have stayed or visited. It is associated with the broader context of the journey and the experiences of the characters.&lt;SEP&gt;A town in Cornwall, England, reached after the journey. It is described as a small, traditional town with a market day, where the narrator meets their unknown friend. The town reflects the Cornish way of life and is central to the narrative's conclusion. It serves as a destination and a place of cultural significance.&lt;SEP&gt;Helstone is a location that the group plans to stop at during their journey. It is mentioned as a place where they wish to stay for an hour or two and find a friend. Helstone is significant as it is part of the route and serves as a point of interest for the group.&lt;SEP&gt;Helstone is a town in Cornwall, mentioned as a possible route during the journey. It is referenced as an alternative to the main road, highlighting the variety of paths available in the region. Helstone is significant in the context of the narrative as it represents a potential stop or route option.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94e0080d8e548382ec3fd09c122e9b19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c527f463c6b54a8cb919a64d12423b24&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59fd7b8af627bbbd404acb5ac88aef98</data>
</node>
<node id="OSMUNDA REGALIS">
  <data key="d0">OSMUNDA REGALIS</data>
  <data key="d1">PLANT</data>
  <data key="d2">Osmunda regalis is a plant species, specifically a fern, mentioned in the text. It is described as growing abundantly in the ferny glade at Trelowarren. This plant is significant in the context of the narrative as it represents the natural flora of the region and is of interest to the characters.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f</data>
</node>
<node id="BLACKBERRIES">
  <data key="d0">BLACKBERRIES</data>
  <data key="d1">FOOD</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of fruit that grows on brambles. They were gathered by the waiter and given to the guests as part of their dinner. The blackberries are an important element in the narrative, symbolizing care and hospitality.&lt;SEP&gt;Blackberries are a fruit mentioned in the text, collected by the two girls during the journey. They are described as large and abundant, and their collection is a highlight of the trip. Blackberries are significant in the context of the narrative as they represent a simple pleasure and a shared activity among the characters.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd2b50b24a25833ce136611502f99de5</data>
</node>
<node id="CARRIAGE">
  <data key="d0">CARRIAGE</data>
  <data key="d1">VEHICLE</data>
  <data key="d2">A carriage is a vehicle mentioned in the text, used by the travelers during their journey. It is described as being lightened after some passengers disembarked. The carriage is significant in the context of the narrative as it represents the mode of transportation and the movement of the characters.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f</data>
</node>
<node id="DRIVER">
  <data key="d0">DRIVER</data>
  <data key="d1">PROFESSION</data>
  <data key="d2">The driver is a character in the text, referred to as 'Charles' in the narrative. He is responsible for driving the carriage and engages in conversation with the narrator. The driver is significant in the context of the narrative as he provides a perspective on life and work in the region.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 9}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 38}]</data>
</node>
<node id="TWO GIRLS">
  <data key="d0">TWO GIRLS</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">The two girls are characters in the text, described as young and enthusiastic about gathering blackberries. They are mentioned as being busy with their activity and returning to the carriage with their baskets. The two girls are significant in the context of the narrative as they represent a moment of joy and simplicity in the journey.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f</data>
</node>
<node id="HORSE">
  <data key="d0">HORSE</data>
  <data key="d1">ANIMAL</data>
  <data key="d2">A Cornish horse known for its 'dogged persistency.' It is described as plodding on mile after mile, showing determination and endurance. The horse is a symbol of the hardworking nature of the Cornish people and contributes to the journey's progression.&lt;SEP&gt;The horse is a character in the text, described as being fed while the carriage waits. It is associated with the driver and is part of the journey's logistics. The horse is significant in the context of the narrative as it represents the means of transportation and the connection between the driver and the environment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 9}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 38}]</data>
</node>
<node id="BABY">
  <data key="d0">BABY</data>
  <data key="d1">ANIMAL</data>
  <data key="d2">The baby is a character in the text, mentioned in the context of the driver's personal loss. It is described as having died, leading to the death of the driver's wife. The baby is significant in the context of the narrative as it represents a tragic event that affects the driver's emotional state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f</data>
</node>
<node id="WIFE">
  <data key="d0">WIFE</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">The wife is a character in the text, mentioned as having died from consumption after the death of the baby. She is described as having broken her heart, leading to her illness and eventual death. The wife is significant in the context of the narrative as she represents a personal tragedy that impacts the driver's life.</data>
