<node id="JOHN CURGENVEN">
  <data key="d0">JOHN CURGENVEN</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A man who is part of the group exploring the Lizard area. He is described as someone who has seen shipwrecks firsthand and is familiar with the dangers of the coast. He provides insight into the role of the coast-guard and the life-boat house.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who is part of the local community and was recognized for their kindness. They were among the attendees at the rectory tea and were seen as a friend to the narrator. Their role in the narrative is to provide a sense of familiarity and warmth in an unfamiliar setting. They are described as being well-known in the area and have a friendly demeanor.&lt;SEP&gt;John Curgenven is a Cornish fisherman described as having a weather-beaten, sharp-lined face with a paternal smile and keen, kindly eyes. He is part of an old family in Cornwall and is known for his kindness to others. His presence in the text is significant as he represents the traditional and cultural identity of the Cornish people. He is also mentioned as being proud of his heritage and has a calm, composed demeanor.&lt;SEP&gt;John Curgenven is a character mentioned as a relative of the landlady or Esther. He is part of the local network of connections and is associated with the group's social interactions. His potential return is a point of anticipation for the group.&lt;SEP&gt;John Curgenven is a character who appears in the narrative, offering assistance to the narrator. He is described as having a 'tender patronising' air, reminiscent of King Arthur. He brings fish as a gift and is portrayed as helpful and courteous. His role is significant as he guides the narrator through the Cornish moor and provides information about the area. He is also associated with the local community and has a deep understanding of the challenges faced by the people of the Lizard.&lt;SEP&gt;John Curgenven is a fisherman who played a central role in rescuing passengers from the wreck of the _Brest_. He is described as having strong arms and being fearless, and he is known for his dedication to his duty. His actions during the rescue demonstrate his professionalism and compassion. He also has a personal injury, a missing finger, which he attributes to the incident. His presence and leadership are significant in the narrative.&lt;SEP&gt;John Curgenven is a person mentioned as a boatman who was not available during the journey. He is described as more skilled than the current boatmen, indicating his importance in the context of local knowledge and navigation. His absence is noted as a disappointment.&lt;SEP&gt;John Curgenven is a person who is involved in the boating excursions planned by the group. He is described as having a boat ready at Church Cove and being chivalrous and careful during the trip. His role is significant as he facilitates the group's access to the sea-caves and helps them navigate the challenging rocks. He is also connected to the group through their shared interest in exploring the coastal area.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94e0080d8e548382ec3fd09c122e9b19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d3c8ddcaba7527f7fcbf3bec3621635&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-000278539400b09a416764f3bc60efe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b862cd9f3d4a2a5394fa477c897d562&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df082bbea6d90a549cd8b56e6131733a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e302b5e22b09ab16666a0e39faaadeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1d2b2559cbc455b5cb2409b4efdaf8e</data>
