  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A location in Cornwall known for its natural beauty and cultural significance. It is mentioned in the context of a garden filled with flowers and a place where the narrator and others attend a tea. It is also noted for its association with longevity, as seen in the register books. This location plays a key role in the social gathering described in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;A location in Cornwall that is referenced as a destination for the group. It is mentioned in the context of the path that guides people between locations during dark nights.&lt;SEP&gt;Kynance is a location in the Cornish landscape, mentioned as a destination for the group. It is part of the natural scenery and is associated with the cliffs and coastal paths. The location is important for the group's travel plans and their exploration of the area.&lt;SEP&gt;Kynance is a location mentioned in the text, possibly a coastal area or a place of interest. It is referenced in the context of a walk that requires careful navigation. The name suggests it could be a natural landmark or a scenic spot, potentially associated with the Lizard Lights mentioned later in the text. Its significance lies in being a destination or a point of reference for the journey.&lt;SEP&gt;Kynance is a location that is mentioned in the context of the waves and the coast. It is described as being two miles off, and its outline is visible under the full moon. Kynance is significant as it is part of the natural landscape that the group experiences during their journey.</data>
