<node id="KENNACK SANDS">
  <data key="d0">KENNACK SANDS</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A coastal area in Cornwall known for its sandy beaches and natural beauty. It is described as resembling a scene from Shakespeare's works, emphasizing its picturesque quality.&lt;SEP&gt;Kennack Sands is a beach where the mother and her children were seen bathing. It is referenced as a location where the children were playing before entering the cave. The sands are described as soft and suitable for barefoot walking, which is significant to the narrative's atmosphere.&lt;SEP&gt;Kennack Sands is a location mentioned in the text, likely a beach or a coastal area. It is part of the planned route for the journey, suggesting it is a place of interest or a destination. The name implies it may be a natural feature, possibly known for its beauty or recreational value. It plays a role in the itinerary of the ladies' travels.</data>
