  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A small fishing village located in Cornwall, England. It is described as a pleasant nook with a mild climate, sheltered from harsh weather, and known for its natural beauty. The village is also noted for its community and the presence of an inn that caters to invalids.&lt;SEP&gt;A small village in Cornwall, mentioned as a destination along the cliff-walk from Poltesco. The text notes that it has some antiquarian interest, though the narrator does not explore it in depth. It is a quiet, rural settlement with historical significance.&lt;SEP&gt;Cadgwith is a coastal village where the rescued passengers were taken after the wreck. It is mentioned as a place where the fishermen's cottages and the parsonage were used to shelter the survivors. Its role in providing temporary refuge underscores its importance in the event.&lt;SEP&gt;Cadgwith is a location mentioned in the text, likely a village or town. It is part of the planned route for the journey, indicating it is a stop or a point of interest. The name suggests it is a small settlement, possibly located near the coast. It serves as a waypoint in the ladies' travel plans.</data>
