</node>
<node id="WATER-PLANTS">
  <data key="d0">WATER-PLANTS</data>
  <data key="d1">PLANT</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of plant that grows near water sources, such as streams or rivulets. The text mentions water-plants as part of the flora collected by the group. These plants are often found in moist environments and contribute to the biodiversity of the area.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-436157d1f1611fd4639ef4c3e3e77cf8</data>
</node>
<node id="CRINGO">
  <data key="d0">CRINGO</data>
  <data key="d1">PLANT</data>
  <data key="d2">A plant with grey-green foliage, commonly found along the coast. The text describes cringo, or sea-holly, as abundant in the area. It is noted for its beauty and is part of the natural landscape of Cornwall.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-436157d1f1611fd4639ef4c3e3e77cf8</data>
</node>
<node id="CORNISHMAN">
  <data key="d0">CORNISHMAN</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who is described as being proud of the Fine Art Exhibition of Ruan Minor. He is referred to as a 'Cornishman' and is mentioned in the context of the author's visit to Cadgwith. His pride in the exhibition is noted, but the author chooses not to wound his feelings by expressing a preference for nature over art.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-445ffbc070c2c6aec738b62a5960af9d</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 6}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 33}]</data>
</node>
<node id="DEVIL'S FRYING-PAN">
  <data key="d0">DEVIL'S FRYING-PAN</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A natural amphitheatre formed by a semi-circular slope covered with grass and flowers. It is described as a wonderful sight, with a shingly beach at the bottom and an arch of serpentine rock. The location is highlighted as a significant natural feature near Cadgwith.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-445ffbc070c2c6aec738b62a5960af9d</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 23}]</data>
</node>
<node id="PARSON">
  <data key="d0">PARSON</data>
  <data key="d1">PROFESSION</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who is described as the parson of Cadgwith. He and his wife are noted for their kindness towards invalids, whether rich or poor. The author mentions that they were looked down upon for not knowing them, indicating their social significance in the area.&lt;SEP&gt;The parson is a clergyman from St. Sennen who is mentioned as having known the long-dead curate. He is described as a middle-aged gentleman who remembers the curate from his youth. His role in the narrative is to provide historical context about the curate and his influence on the community. He is also present at Land's End during the visit, adding to the sense of shared experience.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-445ffbc070c2c6aec738b62a5960af9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9294464d408e527bddad8101fcc42d3</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 20}]</data>
</node>
<node id="FINE ART EXHIBITION OF RUAN MINOR">
  <data key="d0">FINE ART EXHIBITION OF RUAN MINOR</data>
  <data key="d1">EVENT</data>
  <data key="d2">A fine art exhibition featuring the work of Ruan Minor. It is mentioned as not being overwhelmingly attractive to the author, who had previously visited other galleries and museums. The exhibition is referenced in the context of the author's preference for nature over art.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-445ffbc070c2c6aec738b62a5960af9d</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 6}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 33}]</data>
</node>
<node id="MARY MUNDY">
  <data key="d0">MARY MUNDY</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who ran a cottage and interacted with the travelers. She was described as unappreciated by her brother and was disappointed when the travelers did not stay. She expressed a desire for the travelers to return and spoke highly of the professor, even though they were far away. Her role in the narrative is to represent the local community and its hospitality.&lt;SEP&gt;Mary Mundy is a central character in the narrative, admired by the Professor for her beauty and presence. She plays a significant role in the group's journey and their interactions. Her name is mentioned in a poetic line that highlights her importance. She is associated with the setting of the Lizard and is connected to other characters like Esther and John Curgenven.&lt;SEP&gt;Mary Mundy is a central character in the narrative, known for her hospitality and role in providing a warm welcome to the travelers. She is described as a bright, brown-faced woman with red cheeks and black eyes. Her actions include preparing a substantial meal for the visitors and expressing gratitude for their presence. She is also associated with the farm-yard and the Old Inn.&lt;SEP&gt;Mary Mundy is a central figure in the narrative, associated with the Old Inn in Mullion. She is described as the owner of the inn and is known for her hospitality. Her absence causes some concern among the narrator and Charles, highlighting her importance in the local community. Mary is also connected to a poem written by a professor, which adds to her cultural significance in the area. Her presence is crucial for the smooth operation of the inn and the comfort of guests.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e785b114eb81de96943779a460bab162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c13d92c83041a8623279efdde8af1707&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-000278539400b09a416764f3bc60efe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01da0d7fe4c0b05440c961a17b63f8b1</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}]</data>
</node>
<node id="MULLION COVE">
  <data key="d0">MULLION COVE</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Mullion Cove is a coastal area known for its sea-caves and natural beauty. It serves as the primary setting of the narrative, where the characters explore and experience the environment. The cove is described as being enclosed by grand rocks of serpentine and is accessible through a narrow cave. Its significance lies in its role as a place of adventure and reflection.&lt;SEP&gt;Mullion Cove is a coastal location in Cornwall, described as a place with sea-caves and rocky terrain. It is a destination for the narrator's journey and is noted for its natural beauty and unique features. The cove is accessible via a rough road and is associated with the Old Inn in Mullion. Its significance lies in its geographical and scenic value, making it an important stop in the narrative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e785b114eb81de96943779a460bab162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad3c1e9f9f896847e83e5929f169df65</data>
</node>
<node id="OLD INN">
  <data key="d0">OLD INN</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">The Old Inn is a location in Mullion, associated with Mary Mundy. It is described as a comfortable and welcoming place for travelers, and it is noted for its hospitality. The inn is significant in the narrative as it is where the narrator stays and where Mary Mundy is expected to be. The inn's reputation and the care taken by its staff reflect its importance in the local community.&lt;SEP&gt;The Old Inn is a significant location where the travelers arrive after their journey. It is described as a neat little parlour at the back of the inn, where Mary Mundy greets them and serves a substantial meal. The inn is associated with Mary Mundy and the Cornish setting.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e785b114eb81de96943779a460bab162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c13d92c83041a8623279efdde8af1707</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}]</data>
</node>
<node id="MULLION ISLAND">
  <data key="d0">MULLION ISLAND</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Mullion Island is a nearby island located about a quarter of a mile off the coast of Mullion Cove. It is mentioned as a point of interest, though not directly visited in the narrative. Its presence adds to the sense of isolation and natural beauty of the cove.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad3c1e9f9f896847e83e5929f169df65</data>
</node>
<node id="THE MOTHER">
  <data key="d0">THE MOTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">The mother is a character who is anxious about her children's safety. She is described as an 'old hen' and is concerned about her six children who have entered the cave. Her emotional state and actions drive part of the narrative's tension and highlight the risks involved in exploring the cove.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad3c1e9f9f896847e83e5929f169df65</data>
</node>
<node id="THE SMALL BOY">
  <data key="d0">THE SMALL BOY</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">The small boy is one of the mother's children who remains behind while the others enter the cave. He is described as looking as if he wishes he had gone with them, indicating his curiosity and possible regret. His presence adds to the emotional dynamics of the scene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad3c1e9f9f896847e83e5929f169df65</data>
</node>
<node id="THE ARTIST'S WIFE">
  <data key="d0">THE ARTIST'S WIFE</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">The artist's wife is a character who is standing beside her husband's easel while he paints. She is described as wearing a rough serge dress with bare feet, showing a casual and artistic lifestyle. Her presence and interaction with the narrator add a layer of cultural and artistic context to the scene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad3c1e9f9f896847e83e5929f169df65</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 14}]</data>
</node>
<node id="THE ARTIST">
  <data key="d0">THE ARTIST</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">The artist is a character who is painting at the cove. He is described as earnestly working on his artwork, and his painting is of interest to the narrator. His role highlights the artistic and creative aspects of the setting, contributing to the overall ambiance of the narrative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad3c1e9f9f896847e83e5929f169df65</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 14}]</data>
</node>
<node id="FARM-YARD">
  <data key="d0">FARM-YARD</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">The farm-yard is an important location in the story, serving as a place where the travelers encounter a milk-maid and observe the cows. It is described as a picture of an English farm-yard with a regular old-fashioned milk-maid and cows. The farm-yard is connected to Mary Mundy and the Cornish landscape.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c13d92c83041a8623279efdde8af1707</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}]</data>
</node>
<node id="COWS">
  <data key="d0">COWS</data>
  <data key="d1">ANIMAL</data>
  <data key="d2">Cows are an important element in the narrative, particularly in relation to the Cornish landscape. They are described as sleek, calm creatures with Juno-eyed and soft-skinned features. Their color is noted to harmonize with the landscape, and they are linked to the production of cream and butter. The cows are associated with the farm-yard and the Cornish environment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c13d92c83041a8623279efdde8af1707</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}]</data>
</node>
<node id="TEA">
  <data key="d0">TEA</data>
  <data key="d1">FOOD</data>
  <data key="d2">A traditional drink made by the 'honest man's' wife, which is described as being a 'most sumptuous meal.' The tea is a central element of the hospitality offered to the narrator and others, showcasing the warmth and generosity of the household. It plays a key role in the social interaction and is a symbol of the family's kindness and tradition.&lt;SEP&gt;Tea is a key food item in the story, served by Mary Mundy as part of a substantial meal. It is described as a welcome drink for weary travelers, hot, strong, and harmless. Tea is associated with Mary Mundy and the Old Inn.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c13d92c83041a8623279efdde8af1707&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93</data>
</node>
<node id="CLOTTED CREAM">
  <data key="d0">CLOTTED CREAM</data>
  <data key="d1">FOOD</data>
  <data key="d2">Clotted cream is a notable food item in the story, described as thick and rich, with the spoon able to stand upright in it. It is associated with the Cornish cows and is served by Mary Mundy as part of the meal. The cream is linked to the Cornish landscape and the farm-yard.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c13d92c83041a8623279efdde8af1707</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}]</data>
</node>
<node id="PROFESSOR">
  <data key="d0">PROFESSOR</data>
  <data key="d1">PROFESSION</data>
  <data key="d2">A man who was mentioned by Mary Mundy as someone the travelers should greet. He was described as a very nice gentleman, and Mary expressed hope that he would be well. His role in the narrative is to represent a figure of authority or respect in the local community.&lt;SEP&gt;The Professor is an admirer of Mary Mundy, referring to her as 'the brightest thing on Cornish land.' He is a figure of authority and appreciation within the group, contributing to the narrative's tone and perspective. His admiration for Mary Mundy is a key point of interest in the text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-01da0d7fe4c0b05440c961a17b63f8b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-000278539400b09a416764f3bc60efe5</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}]</data>
</node>
<node id="CORNISH">
  <data key="d0">CORNISH</data>
  <data key="d1">NATIONALITY</data>
  <data key="d2">A language historically spoken in Cornwall, which was used in religious services until 1678. After this, it gradually disappeared, and the local population became more English-speaking.&lt;SEP&gt;Cornish refers to the cultural and geographical region in England where the narrative takes place. It is associated with traditions, landscapes, and local folklore, such as the legend of Tregeagle. The term also relates to the nationality of the people in the area.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-000278539400b09a416764f3bc60efe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5dd17ac6c7028198a21adf7ff042f8e</data>
</node>
<node id="TREGEAGLE">
  <data key="d0">TREGEAGLE</data>
  <data key="d1">ANIMAL</data>
  <data key="d2">Tregeagle is a demon from Cornish folklore, associated with the legend of Arthur. He is described as a great demon whose home was sunk to the bottom of the mere after selling his soul to the devil. His presence adds to the supernatural and eerie atmosphere of the narrative, reinforcing the mythological elements of the story.&lt;SEP&gt;Tregeagle is a mythical figure in Cornish tradition, described as a ghostly steward who sold his soul to the devil. He is associated with the legend of Dozmare Pool and is linked to the supernatural elements of the story. His presence adds a layer of mystery and folklore to the narrative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-000278539400b09a416764f3bc60efe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 21}]</data>
</node>
<node id="SUNDAY">
  <data key="d0">SUNDAY</data>
  <data key="d1">DATE</data>
  <data key="d2">Sunday is a date mentioned in the narrative, marking the fourth day of the journey. It is associated with the group's activities, including attending church and enjoying the peaceful atmosphere of the day. The date reflects the passage of time and the rhythm of the journey.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-000278539400b09a416764f3bc60efe5</data>
</node>
<node id="ST. NEOTS">
  <data key="d0">ST. NEOTS</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">St. Neots is a location referenced in the narrative, specifically in relation to the legend of Excalibur being thrown into Dozmare Pool. It is part of the Cornish landscape and is tied to the mythological elements of the story.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-000278539400b09a416764f3bc60efe5</data>
</node>
<node id="EXCALIBUR">
  <data key="d0">EXCALIBUR</data>
  <data key="d1">ARTWORK</data>
  <data key="d2">Excalibur is a legendary sword from Arthurian mythology, mentioned in the context of Dozmare Pool. It is associated with the mythological background of the Cornish region and contributes to the narrative's sense of history and folklore.&lt;SEP&gt;Excalibur is the legendary sword of King Arthur, given to him by the Lady of the Lake. It was thrown into the lake by Sir Bedevere at Arthur's request. Excalibur is a symbol of Arthur's power and destiny, and its fate is closely tied to the legend of his death and possible return.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-000278539400b09a416764f3bc60efe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
</node>
<node id="ESTHER">
  <data key="d0">ESTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Esther is a character who works as a handmaid at the Lizard. She is described as kind and helpful, and she hopes the group will return. Her role is important in providing a warm farewell and maintaining a positive connection with the group. She is also mentioned in the context of the artistic mural decorations in the parlour, which adds to her significance in the narrative.&lt;SEP&gt;Esther is a serving maiden mentioned in the narrative, associated with the group's landlady. She is part of the local community and contributes to the group's experience at the Lizard. Her presence reflects the interconnectedness of the characters in the story.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94e0080d8e548382ec3fd09c122e9b19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-000278539400b09a416764f3bc60efe5</data>
</node>
<node id="LANDEWEDNACK">
  <data key="d0">LANDEWEDNACK</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A location in Cornwall that is mentioned in the context of the rector's previous position. It is associated with the history of the region and the religious leadership. This location is important for understanding the background of the rector and the broader context of the village's religious and social structure.&lt;SEP&gt;A parish in Cornwall, England, associated with the white-roofed church of St. Wednack. It is noted for its historical significance, including the last sermon in Cornish and its connection to the local community.&lt;SEP&gt;Landewednack is a location mentioned in the narrative, associated with a harvest festival. It is described as a place where the community gathers for a thanksgiving service, highlighting its cultural and social significance. The location plays a role in the broader context of the narrative's themes of gratitude and reflection.&lt;SEP&gt;Landewednack is a location that refers to a church. It is mentioned as the group passes by the Church of Landewednack while driving through the Lizard Town. This location is significant as it is part of the scenery and the group's experience during their journey.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94e0080d8e548382ec3fd09c122e9b19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5dd17ac6c7028198a21adf7ff042f8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9baf49d70e5cdf906cbc1334a05b60e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e302b5e22b09ab16666a0e39faaadeb</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 14}]</data>
</node>
<node id="LANDEWEDNACK CHURCH">
  <data key="d0">LANDEWEDNACK CHURCH</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A church located in Landewednack, known for its Norman doorway, hagioscope, and picturesque setting near the sea. It is the most southerly church in England and holds historical significance, including the last sermon in Cornish.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a5dd17ac6c7028198a21adf7ff042f8e</data>
</node>
<node id="THE NARRATOR">
  <data key="d0">THE NARRATOR</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who visited Landewednack and observed the local community, the church, and the natural surroundings. They were part of the group that attended the church service and later explored the area.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a5dd17ac6c7028198a21adf7ff042f8e</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 14}]</data>
</node>
<node id="BLACK DOG">
  <data key="d0">BLACK DOG</data>
  <data key="d1">ANIMAL</data>
  <data key="d2">A large black dog that accompanied the narrator and others to the church service. It behaved well during the service and was described as resembling Highland shepherds' dogs in conduct.</data>
