<node id="ST. SENNEN">
  <data key="d0">ST. SENNEN</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">St. Sennen is a village in Cornwall, England, referenced as the home of the parson who knew the long-dead curate. It is part of the local community and is connected to the history of the curate and his interactions with the children of the parson. The village is a key location in the narrative, representing the cultural and religious background of the area.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9294464d408e527bddad8101fcc42d3</data>
</node>
<node id="MR. ----">
  <data key="d0">MR. ----</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Mr. ---- refers to the long-dead curate of St. Sennen's, who is remembered by the parson. He was a young clergyman who walked across the cliffs with the parson's children and carried books by Southey and Wordsworth. He later married a woman from the north and is now a distant memory. His presence in the narrative highlights the passage of time and the enduring impact of individuals on their communities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9294464d408e527bddad8101fcc42d3</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 20}]</data>
</node>
<node id="GUIDE">
  <data key="d0">GUIDE</data>
  <data key="d1">PROFESSION</data>
  <data key="d2">The guide is a man-of-war's-man who leads the group along the rocks at Land's End. He is described as a regular sailor with a keen eye and a friendly demeanor. He offers assistance to the visitors and shares stories about the area, including the incident involving General Armstrong's horse and the bullock that swam to safety. His role is to ensure the safety of the visitors while providing insight into the local history and geography.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9294464d408e527bddad8101fcc42d3</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 19}]</data>
</node>
<node id="BRISONS">
  <data key="d0">BRISONS</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">The Brisons are a rock formation in the sea near Land's End, where a shipwreck occurred. A captain and his wife were stranded on this rock for two days before being rescued. This event is recounted by the guide and adds to the sense of danger and adventure associated with the location. The Brisons are an important landmark in the narrative, symbolizing both peril and survival.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9294464d408e527bddad8101fcc42d3</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 19}]</data>
</node>
<node id="HE">
  <data key="d0">HE</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who was believed to be drowned but later found swimming unhurt. He is described as being fished up and exhibited as a curiosity. His survival highlights his resilience and the dramatic nature of the event.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921</data>
</node>
<node id="THE BRISONS">
  <data key="d0">THE BRISONS</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A rock in the sea where a shipwreck occurred. It is significant because it was the site where the captain and his wife were stranded for two days before being rescued. This location is central to the story of survival and rescue.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921</data>
</node>
<node id="A SHIP">
  <data key="d0">A SHIP</data>
  <data key="d1">VEHICLE</data>
  <data key="d2">A ship that went to pieces near the Brisons. It is part of a historical event involving the captain and his wife, highlighting the dangers of maritime travel and the importance of rescue operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921</data>
</node>
<node id="THE CAPTAIN">
  <data key="d0">THE CAPTAIN</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">The captain of the ship that wrecked at the Brisons. He survived the ordeal but was deeply affected by the loss of his wife, which led to his mental breakdown. His story illustrates the emotional impact of survival and loss.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921</data>
</node>
<node id="THE CAPTAIN'S WIFE">
  <data key="d0">THE CAPTAIN'S WIFE</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">The wife of the captain who was stranded on the Brisons. She was rescued first but fell into the water, leading to her temporary drowning. Her survival and subsequent emotional trauma highlight the risks of maritime disasters.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921</data>
</node>
<node id="WHITESAND BAY">
  <data key="d0">WHITESAND BAY</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A bay in Cornwall where a woman was caught in a storm and sought refuge at a coastguard station. This event is recounted as a romantic tale, emphasizing the region's history of maritime challenges and human resilience.&lt;SEP&gt;A location that is considered as a potential destination for the group's final drive. It is described as a place with 'a wide sweep of sunshiny sand' and is associated with the idea of relaxation and leisure. The group decides to visit it as a final stop before returning home.&lt;SEP&gt;Whitesand Bay is a coastal area in Cornwall, England, noted for its sandy beach. It is mentioned in the context of being a place that the characters do not often visit. This location is significant as it reflects the contrast between different parts of the Cornish coast and highlights the varied landscapes the travelers encounter during their journey.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3d113b2b3dce6fe310bc06ea08b364cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fb3571cbea81efe6dfda2aa2199fdbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 11}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 43}]</data>
</node>
<node id="THE HONEST SAILOR">
  <data key="d0">THE HONEST SAILOR</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A sailor who shared stories about his experiences aboard H.M.S. Agamemnon. He is described as honest, weather-beaten, and a fine specimen of a British tar. His anecdotes provide insight into the maritime life and historical events.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921</data>
</node>
<node id="H.M.S. AGAMEMNON">
  <data key="d0">H.M.S. AGAMEMNON</data>
  <data key="d1">VEHICLE</data>
  <data key="d2">A ship in which the sailor served as an A.B. (able seaman). It is associated with the Crimean War and the sailor's experiences during that time, linking the narrative to historical military events.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921</data>
</node>
<node id="THE CRIMEAN WAR">
  <data key="d0">THE CRIMEAN WAR</data>
  <data key="d1">EVENT</data>
