  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who is part of the group traveling and is involved in the decision-making process. They express disappointment about not being able to collect shells for a bazaar and suggest visiting Whitesand Bay again. Their emotional state reflects the overall sentiment of the group.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1fb3571cbea81efe6dfda2aa2199fdbe</data>
</node>
<node id="THE CAUTIOUS ONE">
  <data key="d0">THE CAUTIOUS ONE</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who is part of the group and is described as 'cautious.' She raises concerns about the safety of bathing at Sennen Cove and influences the group's decision to avoid the area. Her caution contrasts with the group's initial enthusiasm.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1fb3571cbea81efe6dfda2aa2199fdbe</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 11}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 43}]</data>
</node>
<node id="FISHER WIFE">
  <data key="d0">FISHER WIFE</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A local resident who interacts with the narrator during their visit. She is described as a fisher wife who speaks about her children and their education. She engages in conversation with the narrator and helps resolve a conflict between two children. Her presence highlights the close-knit nature of the community and the importance of family and education in the village.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a5e39286406a99236327034e4840f578</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 28}]</data>
</node>
<node id="BIG MAN (COASTGUARD)">
  <data key="d0">BIG MAN (COASTGUARD)</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A local man who works as a coastguard and assists the narrator by carrying their belongings. He is described as a big man who apologizes for not being clean but still offers help. His interaction with the narrator provides insight into the daily life of the local community and the informal support systems in place.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a5e39286406a99236327034e4840f578</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 25}]</data>
</node>
<node id="SEARCH FOR SHELLS">
  <data key="d0">SEARCH FOR SHELLS</data>
  <data key="d1">EVENT</data>
  <data key="d2">An event involving the search for shells on the beach, which becomes a central activity during the visit. The act of collecting shells is described as fascinating and engaging, highlighting the natural beauty of the location. This event reflects the leisurely and reflective nature of the day spent at the Land's End.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a5e39286406a99236327034e4840f578</data>
</node>
<node id="PRINCESS FREDERICA OF HANOVER">
  <data key="d0">PRINCESS FREDERICA OF HANOVER</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Princess Frederica of Hanover is a royal figure mentioned in the text. She is described as a half-English, half-foreign princess whose story has captured the interest of the British public. This entity is significant as she is the central figure of the royal visit and the focus of public curiosity and speculation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3d113b2b3dce6fe310bc06ea08b364cb</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 1}]</data>
</node>
<node id="BARON VON PAWEL-RAMMINGEN">
  <data key="d0">BARON VON PAWEL-RAMMINGEN</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Baron Von Pawel-Rammingen is the father's secretary of Princess Frederica of Hanover. He is described as a stout mediaeval knight who had loved, wooed, and married her. This entity is significant as he is the husband of the princess and plays a role in the royal visit to St. Michael's Mount.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3d113b2b3dce6fe310bc06ea08b364cb</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 1}]</data>
</node>
<node id="RISTORMEL">
  <data key="d0">RISTORMEL</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Ristormel is one of the ancient castles of Cornwall, mentioned as a fleeting vision during a journey. It represents a historical site with cultural significance in the region. Its role in the narrative is to provide a visual reference point for the setting. Ristormel is associated with the broader history of Cornish castles and their connection to medieval legends.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
</node>
<node id="BODMIN ROAD">
  <data key="d0">BODMIN ROAD</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Bodmin Road is a location where the travelers stopped during their journey. It serves as a practical stopping point in the narrative, indicating the route taken by the characters. Bodmin Road is part of the geographical landscape of Cornwall and is mentioned in the context of travel and exploration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
</node>
<node id="CAMELFORD">
  <data key="d0">CAMELFORD</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Camelford is a town in Cornwall, noted as not being the Camelot of King Arthur. It is described as a modest, commonplace town with typical features like shops and inns. Camelford is associated with the legend of King Arthur, particularly as the site of his final battle and death wound. It is also linked to the story of Arthur's defeat by Mordred.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
</node>
<node id="UTHER PENDRAGON">
  <data key="d0">UTHER PENDRAGON</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Uther Pendragon is a key figure in the Arthurian legends, known for his role in the birth of King Arthur. He is portrayed as a powerful and sometimes brutal leader, contributing to the themes of power and morality in the narrative. His actions and legacy are significant in shaping the Arthurian mythos.&lt;SEP&gt;Uther Pendragon was the King of Britain who fell in love with Ygrayne, the wife of the Duke of Cornwall. He besieged them in their castles, killed the husband, and married Ygrayne. Uther's actions led to the birth of Arthur, who was raised by Sir Ector. Uther's story is foundational to the legend of King Arthur and his lineage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-68ddd89fc902a4696967a79c5aea8055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 12}]</data>
</node>
<node id="YGRAYNE">
  <data key="d0">YGRAYNE</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Ygrayne is a character from Arthurian legend, associated with King Arthur and Tintagel Castle. She is mentioned in the legend of how she was shut up in one of the castles, which is part of the Arthurian mythos. Her story contributes to the romantic and tragic elements of the narrative, linking her to other characters like King Uther Pendragon.&lt;SEP&gt;Ygrayne is a historical figure mentioned in the text, associated with the legend of Tintagel. She is described as the wife of Gorlois and is part of the narrative that connects the castle to the broader history of the region. Her story is intertwined with the fate of the castle and the events that transpired there.&lt;SEP&gt;Ygrayne was the wife of the Duke of Cornwall and the woman Uther Pendragon fell in love with. She was besieged in her castles by Uther, who killed her husband and married her. Ygrayne gave birth to Arthur, who was later raised by Sir Ector. Her role in the legend is crucial to the origin of King Arthur's story.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 12}]</data>
</node>
<node id="MERLIN">
  <data key="d0">MERLIN</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Merlin is a legendary figure mentioned in the text, associated with the story of the 'little naked child' who was 'dear to Heaven.' He is a key character in Arthurian legend and is connected to the mystical and magical aspects of the location. Merlin's role is symbolic of the supernatural and mythical elements of the area.&lt;SEP&gt;Merlin was an enchanter who played a key role in the legend of King Arthur. He advised Uther Pendragon and arranged for Arthur to be taken from the sea-shore beneath Tintagel and raised by Sir Ector. Merlin is a mystical figure associated with Arthur's upbringing and the magical elements of the legend.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 2}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 15}]</data>
</node>
<node id="SIR ECTOR">
  <data key="d0">SIR ECTOR</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Sir Ector was a knight who raised Arthur as his own son after he was taken from the sea-shore beneath Tintagel. He played a vital role in Arthur's early life and education. Sir Ector's relationship with Arthur is central to the legend, as he helped shape Arthur into the future king.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
</node>
<node id="GUINEVERE">
  <data key="d0">GUINEVERE</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Guinevere was the wife of King Arthur and daughter of King Leodegrance. She forsook Arthur for the love of Sir Launcelot, leading to the downfall of Arthur's kingdom. Her story is a key element of the legend, representing the tragic consequences of betrayal and love.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 2}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 16}]</data>
</node>
<node id="SIR LAUNCELOT">
  <data key="d0">SIR LAUNCELOT</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Sir Launcelot was one of Arthur's bravest knights and dearest friends. He fell in love with Guinevere, causing her to forsake Arthur and leading to the disintegration of the Round Table. Sir Launcelot's relationship with both Arthur and Guinevere is central to the legend's tragic arc.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 2}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 16}]</data>
</node>
<node id="MORDRED">
  <data key="d0">MORDRED</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Mordred was Arthur's nephew who raised a rebellion against him and ultimately defeated him at Camelford. His betrayal and victory over Arthur marked the end of Arthur's reign and the beginning of his mysterious disappearance. Mordred's role is pivotal in the legend's conclusion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 2}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 15}]</data>
</node>
<node id="SIR BEDEVERE">
  <data key="d0">SIR BEDEVERE</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Sir Bedevere was Arthur's last faithful knight. He carried Arthur to the shore of a mere and threw his sword Excalibur into the water. Sir Bedevere's loyalty to Arthur is a defining trait in the legend, and his actions mark the final moments of Arthur's life.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 2}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 15}]</data>
</node>
<node id="ORDER OF KNIGHTS OF THE ROUND TABLE">
  <data key="d0">ORDER OF KNIGHTS OF THE ROUND TABLE</data>
  <data key="d1">EVENT</data>
  <data key="d2">The Order of Knights of the Round Table was instituted by King Arthur. These knights were tasked with punishing vice and rescuing oppressed virtue. The Round Table symbolized equality among the knights and was central to Arthur's vision of justice and chivalry.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 2}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 15}]</data>
</node>
<node id="BATTLE OF CAMELFORD">
  <data key="d0">BATTLE OF CAMELFORD</data>
  <data key="d1">EVENT</data>
  <data key="d2">The Battle of Camelford is the site where King Arthur received his death wound and was defeated by Mordred. This event marks the end of Arthur's reign and the beginning of his mysterious disappearance. The battle is a key moment in the legend, symbolizing the fall of a great king.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
</node>
<node id="ARTHUR">
  <data key="d0">ARTHUR</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Arthur is a legendary king of Britain, known for his leadership and the Arthurian legends. He is central to the narrative, symbolizing the mythical past of Cornwall. His role in the story is that of a tragic figure, associated with the legend of Excalibur and his death. He is linked to other characters like Morgane la Faye and the three queens. His story is deeply embedded in Cornish folklore.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 2}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 15}]</data>
</node>
<node id="MORGANE LA FAYE">
  <data key="d0">MORGANE LA FAYE</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Morgane la Faye is a character from Arthurian legend, often depicted as a sorceress and Arthur's sister. She plays a significant role in the legend of Arthur's death, where he is said to have died in her lap. Her presence adds to the mystical and supernatural elements of the narrative, reinforcing the connection between the physical and spiritual worlds in the story.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 2}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 15}]</data>
</node>
<node id="TINTAGEL CASTLE">
  <data key="d0">TINTAGEL CASTLE</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Tintagel Castle is a historic site in Cornwall, England, associated with the legend of King Arthur. It is described as a place of great significance, where Arthur and Ygrayne were connected. The castle is part of the Arthurian mythos and is mentioned in relation to the legend of Ygrayne and King Uther Pendragon. Its location and history make it a key landmark in the narrative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 2}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 15}]</data>
</node>
<node id="KING UTHER PENDRAGON">
  <data key="d0">KING UTHER PENDRAGON</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">King Uther Pendragon is a legendary king of Britain, known for his role in the Arthurian legends. He is associated with the story of Ygrayne, who was married to him after being shut up in a castle. His presence in the narrative reinforces the mythological and historical aspects of the story, connecting him to other figures like Arthur and Ygrayne.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 12}]</data>
</node>
<node id="TRISTRAM">
  <data key="d0">TRISTRAM</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Tristram is a knight from Arthurian legend, known for his love affair with Iseult. He is mentioned in the narrative as part of the story involving King Mark and Iseult. His journey and tragic fate add depth to the Arthurian lore, highlighting themes of love, betrayal, and loss.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 12}]</data>
</node>
<node id="ISEULT">
  <data key="d0">ISEULT</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Iseult is a character from Arthurian legend, known for her love affair with Tristram. She is mentioned in the narrative as part of the story involving King Mark and Tristram. Her role in the legend highlights the themes of love and tragedy, contributing to the emotional depth of the Arthurian myths.&lt;SEP&gt;Iseult is a character from the Arthurian legends, known for her role in the tragic love story with Tristan. She is described as having 'white hands' and is portrayed as innocent, simple, and dignified. Her relationship with Tristan and her connection to the Arthurian legends highlight her significance in the narrative. She is also contrasted with the 'Breton wife,' emphasizing her moral and emotional depth.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-68ddd89fc902a4696967a79c5aea8055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 12}]</data>
</node>
<node id="DELABOLE QUARRIES">
  <data key="d0">DELABOLE QUARRIES</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Delabole quarries are a historical site in Cornwall, known for their slate mining. They are described as an un-sentimental place, contrasting with the romantic and mythical elements of the narrative. Their significance lies in their historical and industrial importance, reflecting the region's cultural and economic history.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 18}]</data>
</node>
<node id="KING MARK">
  <data key="d0">KING MARK</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">King Mark is a central figure in the Arthurian legends, often depicted as a brutal and flawed ruler. He is involved in the tragic love story with Iseult and Tristan, and his actions contribute to the conflict and tragedy in the narrative. His role highlights the moral complexities and societal norms of the time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-68ddd89fc902a4696967a79c5aea8055</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 12}]</data>
</node>
<node id="BOSSINNEY COVE">
  <data key="d0">BOSSINNEY COVE</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Bossinney Cove is a coastal location in Cornwall, England, where the narrator and their companions visit. It is described as a 'pretty cove' with a unique landscape and a memorable encounter with an old man and his donkeys. Its significance lies in its natural beauty and the human interactions that take place there.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-68ddd89fc902a4696967a79c5aea8055</data>
</node>
<node id="ARTHURIAN LEGENDS">
  <data key="d0">ARTHURIAN LEGENDS</data>
  <data key="d1">EVENT</data>
  <data key="d2">The Arthurian legends are a collection of stories and myths surrounding King Arthur and his court. They are central to the narrative, providing a backdrop for the characters and events described. These legends reflect the values and ideals of chivalry, honor, and morality, which are contrasted with the more brutal aspects of the story.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-68ddd89fc902a4696967a79c5aea8055</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 12}]</data>
</node>
<node id="BOTTRIEUX CASTLE">
  <data key="d0">BOTTRIEUX CASTLE</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Bottrieux Castle is an ancient structure mentioned in the text, now reduced to a green mound. It is one of the relics of antiquity found at the location, and it is associated with the broader historical context of the area. The castle's remains suggest a once-mighty fortress that has since been eroded by time and nature.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da</data>
</node>
<node id="FERRABURY CHURCH">
  <data key="d0">FERRABURY CHURCH</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Ferrabury Church is another relic of antiquity mentioned in the text, noted for its silent tower. It is part of the historical landscape and is connected to the legend of the lost bells. The church is described as having a long-standing presence in the area, and its tower is said to be mute after the tragic event involving the ship and the storm.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da</data>
</node>
<node id="GORLOIS">
  <data key="d0">GORLOIS</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Gorlois is a historical figure mentioned in the text, associated with the legend of Tintagel. He is described as the husband of Ygrayne and is linked to the events that led to the fall of the castle. His story is part of the broader narrative of the region's history and the legends surrounding it.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da</data>
</node>
<node id="CHOUGH">
  <data key="d0">CHOUGH</data>
  <data key="d1">ANIMAL</data>
  <data key="d2">A chough is a bird species mentioned in the text, believed to be the reincarnation of King Arthur's soul. It is described as a rare and almost extinct species that lives among the ruins of Tintagel. The bird is associated with local superstitions and is protected by the people of the area.&lt;SEP&gt;A chough is a type of bird, specifically a member of the crow family. It is mentioned as a rare and superstitious creature in the text, with local people avoiding shooting it. The chough is significant due to its association with King Arthur's legend and its symbolic presence in the landscape. Its survival is attributed to local beliefs and reverence.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 6}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 33}]</data>
</node>
<node id="RAVEN">
  <data key="d0">RAVEN</data>
  <data key="d1">ANIMAL</data>
  <data key="d2">A raven is a bird mentioned in the text, associated with the ruins of Tintagel. It is described as a common sight among the ruins and is part of the local wildlife. Ravens are also linked to the superstitions of the area, where they are not hunted or disturbed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da</data>
</node>
<node id="PORTH HERN">
  <data key="d0">PORTH HERN</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Porth Hern is a location in Cornwall, described as a rocky hollow where a stream flows into the sea. It is associated with the choughs and the sea-caves visited by the narrator. The location is significant for its natural beauty and its role in the narrative of the journey.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36</data>
</node>
<node id="TWIN RUINS">
  <data key="d0">TWIN RUINS</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">The twin ruins refer to the remnants of a structure or buildings, likely associated with Tintagel. They are described as places of solitude and silence, contributing to the mystical atmosphere of the location. Their significance lies in their historical and cultural value.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 6}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 33}]</data>
</node>
<node id="LAUNCESTON">
  <data key="d0">LAUNCESTON</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Launceston is a town in Cornwall, England, mentioned in the context of a journey between it and Tintagel. It serves as a reference point for the location of King Arthur's grave. While not elaborated upon in detail, it is an important geographical marker in the narrative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9dca5f3a8de43d3e2bc3bc88b3703f62</data>
</node>
<node id="OLIVER CROMWELL">
  <data key="d0">OLIVER CROMWELL</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Oliver Cromwell was a prominent English military and political leader during the 17th century. In the text, he is referenced as an example of how historical figures can be remembered in multiple forms, similar to the varied accounts of King Arthur's grave. This comparison emphasizes the idea of historical memory and the ways in which legends evolve over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9dca5f3a8de43d3e2bc3bc88b3703f62</data>
</node>
<node id="DINAH MARIA CRAIK">
  <data key="d0">DINAH MARIA CRAIK</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Dinah Maria Craik is the author of the book 'An Unsentimental Journey through Cornwall.' She is mentioned at the end of the text, indicating her role as the writer of the narrative. Her perspective shapes the description of the journey and the reflections on the landscapes and historical figures encountered.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9dca5f3a8de43d3e2bc3bc88b3703f62</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 24}]</data>
