</edge>
<edge source="DESTRUCTION OF THE TAY BRIDGE" target="SALTASH BRIDGE">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The destruction of the Tay Bridge has a second-order relationship with the Saltash Bridge. The former is a tragic event that highlights the dangers of natural disasters, while the latter is a safe and stable structure. This relationship emphasizes the contrast between the two bridges and the importance of engineering and safety.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GERRANS" target="OLD GENTLEMAN">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The travelers' decision to avoid Gerrans is influenced by the old gentleman's advice, even though they ultimately choose not to visit the village. This is a third-order relationship, as the advice indirectly impacts their travel plans. The relationship highlights the travelers' internal conflict between following advice and making independent choices.</data>
