  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CHARLES" target="LADIES">
  <data key="d5">2.4000000000000004</data>
  <data key="d6">Charles has a direct relationship with the ladies in the narrative, as he appears at their door to inquire about and sympathize with them. This relationship reflects a sense of care and concern, as well as a personal connection. The interaction adds a positive note to the otherwise challenging weather conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Charles provides assistance to the ladies during their journey, ensuring their safety and comfort. This relationship is characterized by kindness and dedication. Charles acts as a guide and protector, helping the ladies navigate difficult paths and plan their travel. His actions reflect a strong sense of responsibility and care for their well-being.&lt;SEP&gt;The ladies rely on Charles for transportation and guidance during their journey. This relationship is built on trust and mutual benefit. Charles offers his services as a driver, and the ladies depend on him for their movement and safety. The relationship highlights the importance of cooperation and support in navigating unfamiliar terrain.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-9baf49d70e5cdf906cbc1334a05b60e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ee024234b415f45ea0006087720bb09</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CHARLES" target="MARY MUNDY">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">Charles and Mary Mundy share a professional and personal relationship. Charles is associated with the Old Inn and is familiar with Mary, who is the owner. He often refers to her and ensures that she is present to accommodate guests. This relationship is significant because it affects the availability of services at the inn and the comfort of the narrator. Their connection is further emphasized by the fact that Mary's absence causes some inconvenience, showing her critical role in the operation of the inn.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-e785b114eb81de96943779a460bab162</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CHARLES" target="NARRATOR">
  <data key="d5">1.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The narrator has a direct relationship with Charles, as he is their guide and facilitator during the journey. Charles arranges transportation and ensures the narrator's comfort, indicating a practical and supportive relationship. This relationship is essential for the narrator's experience in Cornwall, as Charles provides access to locations and information about the area. The narrator relies on Charles for navigation and assistance throughout the trip.&lt;SEP&gt;The relationship between Charles and the narrator involves guidance and support during the journey. Charles provides practical assistance, such as suggesting a guide and helping navigate the terrain. This relationship is based on trust and shared experience.</data>
