  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the young folks and the rocks represents a sense of adventure and physical challenge. The young folks enjoy scrambling over the rocks, finding the activity exciting and enjoyable. This relationship illustrates the importance of physical exploration and the thrill of overcoming challenges.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-3cff43d4c0b87d4d234e1ded07f8597b</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="BOAT">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the narrator and the boat reflects a sense of trust and reliance. The narrator relies on the boat to navigate the challenging waters of the Cornish coast and to enjoy the experience of rowing. This relationship highlights the importance of the boat in facilitating the group's activities and ensuring their safety.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-3cff43d4c0b87d4d234e1ded07f8597b</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="SEA">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the narrator and the sea reflects a sense of awe and admiration. The narrator is captivated by the vastness and beauty of the sea, which is described as having a blueness that rivals the Mediterranean. This relationship highlights the narrator's deep appreciation for the natural world and their ability to find beauty in the environment.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-3cff43d4c0b87d4d234e1ded07f8597b</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="OLD INN">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the narrator and the Old Inn is indirect but significant. The narrator plans to stay at the inn and expects Mary Mundy to be present. The inn's role in providing accommodation and hospitality is central to the narrator's experience. This relationship is influenced by the presence or absence of Mary Mundy, highlighting the interdependence between the narrator and the inn's operations.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-e785b114eb81de96943779a460bab162</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="SUNSET">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The narrator observes the sunset from the cliffs, experiencing a deep emotional connection with the natural beauty around them. This relationship highlights the narrator's introspective nature and appreciation for the transient moments of life.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-4223cdc7e9877a27944679ddd0c14739</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="LOVERS">
  <data key="d5">1.2999999999999998</data>
  <data key="d6">The narrator watches the lovers from a distance, feeling a sense of loneliness and admiration for their connection. This relationship illustrates the narrator's reflective nature and the bittersweet nature of observing others' happiness.&lt;SEP&gt;The narrator's observation of the lovers leads to a deeper reflection on the fleeting nature of love and the passage of time. This relationship illustrates the indirect influence of the lovers on the narrator's thoughts and emotions.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-4223cdc7e9877a27944679ddd0c14739</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="SILENCE">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The narrator feels a sense of solitude while watching the sunset, which is intensified by the silence of the environment. This relationship reflects the emotional state of the narrator and the impact of the natural surroundings on their mood.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-4223cdc7e9877a27944679ddd0c14739</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="THE HORSE">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The horse is observed by the narrator and others, forming a connection through the act of watching and interacting with it. This relationship is indirect, as the horse does not directly influence the other entities but serves as a point of interest and companionship. The horse's presence adds to the atmosphere of the scene and provides a contrast to the more active elements of the environment.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-0c276072b502ce264a92ee400fbb2def</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="ROBINSON CRUSOE">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The Robinson Crusoe fish is found in a small pool, and its presence is connected to the narrator's attempt to catch it. This relationship is direct, as the fish is the subject of the narrator's attention and effort. The fish's elusiveness and contentment add to the narrative's themes of patience and the unpredictability of nature.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-0c276072b502ce264a92ee400fbb2def</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="JOHN CURGENVEN">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">John Curgenven offers guidance and assistance to the narrator, helping them navigate the Cornish moor and providing information about Kynance Cove. This relationship is based on mutual respect and trust, with John acting as a protector and guide. The impact of this relationship is significant, as it allows the narrator to explore the area safely and gain insights into the local culture and environment.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-e1d2b2559cbc455b5cb2409b4efdaf8e</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="MOONLIGHT">
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
  <data key="d6">The moonlight indirectly influences the behavior of the characters, including the young people who walk home and the narrator's decision to leave the scene. This second-order relationship highlights how the moonlight affects the broader context of the narrative, influencing the actions and decisions of multiple entities.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-e1d2b2559cbc455b5cb2409b4efdaf8e</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="MY DAUGHTERS">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the narrator and the old gentleman influences the narrator's perspective on the behavior of the young people, particularly the narrator's daughters. This relationship indirectly affects how the narrator interacts with and advises his daughters.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c527f463c6b54a8cb919a64d12423b24</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="TIDE">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the narrator and the tide is one of challenge and adaptation. The advancing tide forces the narrator and others to make quick decisions, affecting their plans and interactions with the environment.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c527f463c6b54a8cb919a64d12423b24</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="LOGAN">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the Logan stone and the narrator is one of curiosity and awe. The narrator reads about the stone since childhood and is eager to see it in person. This relationship is influenced by the historical event involving Lieutenant Goldsmith, which altered the stone's condition.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-2ca4427317d91b282aad105e4c1fcb34</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="METHODIST">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the narrator and the religious groups (Methodist, Quaker, and Church of England) reflects the cultural and spiritual context of Cornwall. The narrator observes the religious practices of the region, noting the prevalence of nonconformist sects and the conservative nature of the Church of England. This relationship highlights the influence of religion on daily life in the area.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-2ca4427317d91b282aad105e4c1fcb34</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="LIEUTENANT GOLDSMITH">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the narrator and the historical event involving Lieutenant Goldsmith is indirect but impactful. The narrator learns about the incident through stories and the altered state of the Logan stone. This relationship connects the narrator to the history of the region and the consequences of human interference with natural landmarks.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-2ca4427317d91b282aad105e4c1fcb34</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="BIG MAN (COASTGUARD)">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the narrator and the big man (coastguard) is one of mutual assistance. The narrator asks him to carry their belongings, and he agrees despite his initial reluctance. This interaction reflects the informal and helpful nature of the local community. The relationship is direct and practical, centered around the needs of the visitor.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-a5e39286406a99236327034e4840f578</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="FISHER WIFE">
  <data key="d5">1.1</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the fisher wife and the narrator is one of friendly conversation and shared interests. They discuss children, education, and daily life, forming a brief but meaningful connection. This relationship highlights the social dynamics of the local community and the importance of personal interactions in a small village.&lt;SEP&gt;The relationship between the narrator and the fisher wife evolves into a second-order relationship through the intervention in the children's conflict. The narrator's action of intervening and resolving the dispute creates a deeper connection between them, reflecting the broader social dynamics of the community and the importance of mediation in maintaining harmony.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-a5e39286406a99236327034e4840f578</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="LOCAL COMMUNITY">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the narrator and the local community is indirect but significant. The narrator's experience at the Land's End and Sennen is shaped by their interactions with the fisher wife and the coastguard, which reflect the broader cultural and social context of the area. This relationship influences the narrator's perception of the place and its people.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-a5e39286406a99236327034e4840f578</data>
  <data key="d8">3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="SEA-CAVES">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the sea-caves and the narrator is one of awe and fascination. The caves are described as a place of mystery and danger, offering a profound experience that leaves a lasting impression. This relationship reflects the emotional and sensory impact of the caves on the narrator, enhancing the narrative of the journey.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="BOAT" target="_BREST_">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">The boat was used by John Curgenven to transport the rescued passengers from the wreck of the _Brest_ to Cadgwith. This is a direct relationship, as the boat served as the primary vehicle for the rescue. The weight of this relationship is high due to the critical role the boat played in the event.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-9b862cd9f3d4a2a5394fa477c897d562</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="JOHN CURGENVEN" target="CORNISH CHOUGH">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">John Curgenven is connected to the Cornish chough through local folklore, which suggests that King Arthur may haunt the coast in the form of this bird. This relationship reflects the cultural and mythical significance of both the individual and the animal, linking them through legend and tradition.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-df082bbea6d90a549cd8b56e6131733a</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="JOHN CURGENVEN" target="TENNYSON">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">Tennyson's depiction of King Arthur influences the perception of John Curgenven, as the text suggests that the fisherman may embody the ideal knight described by the poet. This relationship reflects the literary and cultural impact of Tennyson's work on the narrative and the character's interpretation.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-df082bbea6d90a549cd8b56e6131733a</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="JOHN CURGENVEN" target="GROUP">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the group and John Curgenven is one of trust and assistance. John provides the boat and helps the group navigate the challenging rocks to reach the sea-caves. This relationship is significant because it allows the group to overcome obstacles and achieve their goal of visiting the caves. The impact of this relationship is that it ensures the success of the boating excursion.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-94e0080d8e548382ec3fd09c122e9b19</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="JOHN CURGENVEN" target="_BREST_">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">John Curgenven directly rescued the passengers of the _Brest_ during the shipwreck. This relationship is a direct one, as it involves immediate action and interaction between the two entities. The impact of this relationship is significant, as it saved many lives and demonstrated John's bravery and commitment to his profession.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-9b862cd9f3d4a2a5394fa477c897d562</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="JOHN CURGENVEN" target="COASTGUARD">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The coastguard relied on the help of fishermen like John Curgenven during the rescue of the _Brest_ survivors. This is a direct relationship, as the coastguard and fishermen worked together to save lives. The impact of this relationship is significant, as it highlights the collaboration between different groups in emergency situations.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-9b862cd9f3d4a2a5394fa477c897d562</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="KYNANCE" target="KENNACK SANDS">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The journey involves multiple locations, including Kynance, Kennack Sands, Cadgwith, and Mullion. These locations are connected through the planned route, forming a network of destinations. The relationship between these locations is defined by their geographical proximity and the sequence of the journey. Each location serves as a stop or a point of interest in the overall travel experience.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-5ee024234b415f45ea0006087720bb09</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="COAST-GUARD" target="LIFE-BOAT HOUSE">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the Life-Boat House and the Coast-Guard. The Life-Boat House is where the life-boat is kept, and it is an essential facility for the Coast-Guard's operations. The Life-Boat House serves as a base for rescue efforts during shipwrecks.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-2d3c8ddcaba7527f7fcbf3bec3621635</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="COAST-GUARD" target="PARSON">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the Coast-Guard and the parson. The parson is often at the head of the Coast-Guard when there is a wreck, indicating a close collaboration between religious and maritime authorities in the region. This relationship reflects the community's reliance on both spiritual and practical support during emergencies.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-2d3c8ddcaba7527f7fcbf3bec3621635</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LIZARD LIGHTS" target="SHIPS">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the Lizard Lights and the ships navigating the coast. The Lizard Lights serve as a critical navigational aid, guiding ships through dark and dangerous waters. This relationship is essential for preventing shipwrecks and ensuring safe passage.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-2d3c8ddcaba7527f7fcbf3bec3621635</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LIZARD LIGHTS" target="FOG-HORN">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the Lizard Lights and the fog-horn. Both the Lizard Lights and the fog-horn are powered by the same steam-engine, and they both serve the purpose of guiding ships through dangerous conditions. This relationship highlights the interconnected systems used for maritime safety.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-2d3c8ddcaba7527f7fcbf3bec3621635</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LIZARD LIGHTS" target="YOUNG MAN">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The young man at the Lizard Lights is directly connected to the Lizard Lights through his role as an explainer and operator. He is responsible for demonstrating the technology and providing information about its functions. This relationship reflects the professional and educational aspect of the interaction.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-5d3bdb332242dccc51989976c4f0ce2e</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LIZARD LIGHTS" target="LUMINOUS CIRCLE">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The luminous circle has a second-order relationship with the Lizard Lights, as both are natural and technological phenomena that evoke a sense of awe and curiosity. The presence of the luminous circle may have influenced the decision to visit the Lizard Lights, creating a connection between the two events.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-5d3bdb332242dccc51989976c4f0ce2e</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="KENNACK SANDS" target="CADGWITH">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The journey includes a series of stops at different locations, such as Kennack Sands, Cadgwith, and Mullion. These locations are interconnected through the planned route, forming a chain of destinations. The relationship between these locations is defined by their sequential order and the purpose of the journey. Each location contributes to the overall experience of the ladies' travels.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-5ee024234b415f45ea0006087720bb09</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CADGWITH" target="POLTESCO">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The presence of the cliff-walk from Poltesco to Cadgwith influences the travel plans of the group, encouraging them to explore the coastal landscape. This relationship highlights the interplay between geography and human activity.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-436157d1f1611fd4639ef4c3e3e77cf8</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CADGWITH" target="DEVIL'S FRYING-PAN">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The Devil's Frying-Pan is directly connected to Cadgwith, as it is a natural feature located near the village. The author describes the site in detail, emphasizing its natural beauty and the dramatic contrast between the summer tourist life and the winter hardships faced by the villagers.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-445ffbc070c2c6aec738b62a5960af9d</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CADGWITH" target="PARSON">
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
  <data key="d6">The parson has a second-order relationship with the villagers of Cadgwith, as he and his wife are described as kind individuals who look after invalids. Their actions reflect the broader social dynamics of the village, where community support plays a vital role in daily life.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-445ffbc070c2c6aec738b62a5960af9d</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CADGWITH" target="_BREST_">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The wreck of the _Brest_ led to the temporary sheltering of the survivors in Cadgwith. This is a second-order relationship, as the event indirectly caused the use of Cadgwith as a refuge. The impact of this relationship is moderate, as it shows how the event affected the local community.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-9b862cd9f3d4a2a5394fa477c897d562</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="HARVEST MOON" target="SILENT PEACE">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The harvest moon is associated with a sense of peace and transformation, as it is described as wrapping one in a 'silent peace' and replacing 'the spirit of heaviness.' This relationship highlights the emotional and symbolic impact of the moon on the characters' experience, creating a contrast between the previous state of worry and the current state of tranquility.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-0a11868bbedb00db5ad94f79da20acdd</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="HARVEST MOON" target="GARMENT OF PRAISE">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The harvest moon influences the characters' perception of their surroundings, making them feel as if they are in a 'garment of praise.' This relationship reflects the moon's role in altering the mood and atmosphere of the journey, contributing to a deeper appreciation of the natural beauty and serenity of the Lizard.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-0a11868bbedb00db5ad94f79da20acdd</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="WILD FLOWERS" target="ROOM">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the wild flowers and the transformed room. The wild flowers, placed in jars, significantly changed the appearance of the room, making it more inviting and aesthetically pleasing.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-12e1d0ddbd52f7c9b5db9fe78dbfee83</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CLIFFS" target="LOVERS">
  <data key="d5">0.5</data>
  <data key="d6">The presence of the lovers on the cliffs influences the narrator's perception of the natural setting, adding a layer of emotional complexity to the scene. This relationship shows how human interactions can affect the interpretation of natural beauty.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-4223cdc7e9877a27944679ddd0c14739</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="SEA" target="SUNSET">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The sunset and the sea are connected through the visual spectacle of the golden mist and the reflection of the sun on the water. This relationship emphasizes the harmony between the natural elements and the emotional impact they have on the observer.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-4223cdc7e9877a27944679ddd0c14739</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CORNISHMAN" target="FINE ART EXHIBITION OF RUAN MINOR">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The Cornishman has a direct relationship with the Fine Art Exhibition of Ruan Minor, as he is proud of it and the author feels compelled to explain their preference for nature over art to avoid wounding his feelings. This relationship reflects the personal connection and pride the Cornishman has in the exhibition.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-445ffbc070c2c6aec738b62a5960af9d</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CORNISHMAN" target="CHOUGH">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the chough and the local Cornish people is one of reverence and protection. Due to superstitions, the Cornish people avoid shooting the chough, allowing it to live and breed in peace. This relationship highlights the cultural and spiritual significance of the chough in the region, influencing its survival and coexistence with humans.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="PARSON" target="MR. ----">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the parson and Mr. ---- is one of historical familiarity. The parson recalls knowing Mr. ---- as a young clergyman who often visited the area and interacted with the parson's children. This relationship reflects the deep-rooted connections within the local community and the lasting impact of individuals on future generations. The parson's recollection of Mr. ---- highlights the passage of time and the continuity of tradition.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-b9294464d408e527bddad8101fcc42d3</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="PARSON" target="VISITORS">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the parson and the visitors is one of shared experience and cultural exchange. The parson provides historical context about the curate and the local area, enriching the visitors' understanding of the place they are visiting. This relationship is rooted in the commonality of observing the landscape and reflecting on its significance. It also highlights the role of local figures in preserving and transmitting cultural heritage.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-b9294464d408e527bddad8101fcc42d3</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MARY MUNDY" target="FARM-YARD">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">Mary Mundy has a direct relationship with the farm-yard, as she is the one who prepares the meal and welcomes the travelers there. This relationship is significant because it shows her role as a host and her connection to the Cornish landscape. The farm-yard is a place where she exerts her influence and hospitality.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c13d92c83041a8623279efdde8af1707</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MARY MUNDY" target="OLD INN">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">Mary Mundy has a direct relationship with the Old Inn, as she is the one who greets the travelers and serves them a meal. This relationship is significant because it shows her role as a host and her connection to the Cornish setting. The Old Inn is a place where she exerts her influence and hospitality.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c13d92c83041a8623279efdde8af1707</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MARY MUNDY" target="PROFESSOR">
  <data key="d5">2.2</data>
  <data key="d6">Mary Mundy expressed a desire for the travelers to visit the professor, indicating a connection between the local community and an external figure of authority. This relationship shows how local residents maintain ties with distant individuals.&lt;SEP&gt;Mary Mundy is connected to the Professor through admiration and poetic reference. This relationship is direct and emphasizes her importance in the narrative. It shapes the group's perception of her and contributes to the emotional tone of the story.&lt;SEP&gt;The Professor admires Mary Mundy, highlighting her beauty and significance in the narrative. This relationship is direct and reflects the admiration and respect the Professor has for her. It influences the group's perception of her and contributes to the romantic and poetic tone of the text.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-01da0d7fe4c0b05440c961a17b63f8b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-000278539400b09a416764f3bc60efe5</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MARY MUNDY" target="BROTHER">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
