</edge>
<edge source="BEADLE" target="TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The beadle's indifference to the historical value of the church contrasts with the travelers' appreciation of its architecture. This relationship highlights the difference in perception between locals and outsiders regarding historical sites.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-01da0d7fe4c0b05440c961a17b63f8b1</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="YOUNG WOMAN" target="TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the travelers and the young woman involves a direct exchange of goods (apples) and a brief conversation. The young woman is generous and allows the travelers to take apples freely, while the travelers express gratitude and offer payment. This relationship highlights the hospitality and generosity of the Cornish people.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="YOUNG WOMAN" target="HONEST CORNISHMAN">
  <data key="d5">0.5</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the honest Cornishman and the young woman is indirect, as they are not directly connected in the text. However, the young woman's actions and the Cornishman's behavior suggest a shared cultural background and mutual respect for the Cornish way of life. This second-order relationship reflects the broader social fabric of the region.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="HONEST CORNISHMAN" target="FAMILY">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the honest Cornishman and the family he once served is direct and deeply rooted. He expresses a strong emotional bond and willingness to help them, even if it means traveling long distances. This relationship demonstrates loyalty, devotion, and the lasting impact of professional and personal connections.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="HONEST CORNISHMAN" target="MIDDLE-AGED, COMELY MATRON">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the honest Cornishman and the middle-aged, comely matron is direct and familial. They are married, and she supports him in his hospitality towards the travelers. Their relationship is characterized by warmth, cooperation, and shared responsibility in their home life.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MY WIFE" target="THE HONEST MAN">
  <data key="d5">1.65</data>
  <data key="d6">The 'honest man' and his wife share a deep, affectionate bond, characterized by mutual respect and companionship. Their relationship is described as one of enduring love and partnership, with the wife supporting her husband in his endeavors and the husband showing unwavering devotion to her. This relationship is central to the narrative, as it highlights the emotional core of the story and the values of loyalty and fidelity.&lt;SEP&gt;The 'honest man' and the narrator's wife share a relationship that is defined by mutual respect and appreciation. The 'honest man' expresses admiration for the wife's ability to make the best cup of tea in all Cornwall, and she supports him in his daily activities. This relationship is characterized by a sense of harmony and cooperation, reflecting the broader themes of community and hospitality in the narrative.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="THE HONEST MAN" target="LIGHTHOUSE">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">The 'honest man' has a strong, personal connection to the lighthouse, which he helped build and was lit by the Emperor. This relationship is rooted in a sense of pride and accomplishment, as the lighthouse represents a significant achievement in his life. The lighthouse also symbolizes his connection to the narrator's family, as they were involved in its construction and the event of its lighting. This relationship underscores the historical and emotional significance of the lighthouse in the narrative.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="THE HONEST MAN" target="NARRATOR'S FAMILY">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The 'honest man' maintains a close relationship with the narrator's family, particularly with the narrator's father and uncle. This relationship is marked by a sense of loyalty and gratitude, as the 'honest man' recalls the family's support and the shared experience of lighting the lighthouse. The family's presence in the narrative reinforces the theme of familial bonds and the lasting impact of their connection to the 'honest man.'</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="THE HONEST MAN" target="WAX CANDLE">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The 'honest man' has a symbolic relationship with the wax candle, which he has preserved for nearly thirty years. This relationship is based on the candle's historical and sentimental value, as it represents the moment when the Emperor lit the lighthouse. The candle serves as a physical reminder of the 'honest man's' connection to the family and the significance of the event. This relationship highlights the emotional depth of the 'honest man's' character and his dedication to preserving memories.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ST. AUBYNS" target="PRINCESS FREDERICA OF HANOVER">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the Princess Frederica of Hanover and the St. Aubyns is one of hospitality and social engagement. The St. Aubyns are hosting the royal visit, which indicates a formal and respectful relationship. This relationship is significant as it involves the local aristocracy and the royal family, highlighting the social dynamics and expectations of the time.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-3d113b2b3dce6fe310bc06ea08b364cb</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="WAITER" target="GUESTS">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">The waiter had a direct relationship with the guests, showing care and attentiveness by providing them with blackberries and preventing another guest from taking food. This relationship reflects a professional and friendly interaction.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-bd2b50b24a25833ce136611502f99de5</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="BRAMBLES" target="CHARACTERS">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The brambles, gorse, and heather are indirectly related to the characters, as they form part of the landscape around Land's End. These plants contribute to the natural setting and the overall experience of the journey.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-bd2b50b24a25833ce136611502f99de5</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CORNISHWOMAN" target="CORNISH COW">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The Cornishwoman and the Cornish cow are directly related, as the cow is milked by the woman. Their relationship is part of the local agricultural scene and contributes to the cultural context of the narrative.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-bd2b50b24a25833ce136611502f99de5</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="INHABITANT" target="PRAYER-MEETING">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The inhabitant's attendance at the prayer-meeting influences his daily routine and sense of duty. This relationship highlights the impact of religious practices on his behavior and time management.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c07a1f8303c88520a6e687ca76451218</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="INHABITANT" target="SHOP">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The inhabitant's shop is directly connected to his professional background and current lifestyle. This relationship illustrates how his past career continues to influence his present activities and interactions.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c07a1f8303c88520a6e687ca76451218</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="INHABITANT" target="METHODIST">
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
  <data key="d6">The inhabitant's religious affiliation with the Methodist church shapes his values and social interactions. This relationship reflects the broader cultural and spiritual context of the town.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c07a1f8303c88520a6e687ca76451218</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="INHABITANT" target="TOWN HALL">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The Town Hall in St. Ives is a key location that the inhabitant shows to the narrator, indicating its significance in the town's civic life. This relationship highlights the inhabitant's pride in his community and its landmarks.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c07a1f8303c88520a6e687ca76451218</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="SHOP" target="YOUNG CORNISHMAN">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The young Cornishman's mischief has a direct effect on the environment and the people around him, such as nearly causing damage to the shop. This relationship illustrates the impact of individual actions on the surroundings and the community. The child's behavior is a source of both amusement and concern.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-9baf49d70e5cdf906cbc1334a05b60e0</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GUIDE" target="VISITORS">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the guide and the visitors is one of professional guidance and mutual trust. The guide leads the visitors along the dangerous path at Land's End, ensuring their safety while sharing stories about the area. This relationship is built on the guide's expertise and the visitors' reliance on his knowledge. It also reflects the adventurous spirit of the journey and the importance of local knowledge in navigating challenging environments.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-b9294464d408e527bddad8101fcc42d3</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GUIDE" target="BRISONS">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the guide and the Brisons is one of direct interaction and storytelling. The guide recounts the story of the shipwreck at the Brisons, linking the physical location to a historical event. This relationship emphasizes the guide's role as a storyteller and educator, using the landscape to convey lessons about history and survival. It also illustrates how natural landmarks can become symbols of human experiences.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-b9294464d408e527bddad8101fcc42d3</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="BRISONS" target="SHIPWRECK">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the Brisons and the shipwreck is one of historical significance. The Brisons are the site of a past maritime disaster, where a shipwreck led to the survival of the captain and his wife. This relationship underscores the dangers of the sea and the resilience of those who face them. It also serves as a reminder of the unpredictable nature of the environment and the importance of caution.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-b9294464d408e527bddad8101fcc42d3</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="THE BRISONS" target="THE CAPTAIN">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">The captain and his wife were stranded on the Brisons after their ship wrecked. They held on for two days and nights before being rescued, showcasing their survival skills and the danger of the sea.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="THE BRISONS" target="THE CAPTAIN'S WIFE">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The captain's wife was rescued first using rockets and a rope, but she fell into the water and was temporarily drowned. This event had a profound emotional impact on the captain, leading to his mental breakdown.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="THE CAPTAIN" target="THE CAPTAIN'S WIFE">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The captain's wife's survival and eventual death had a direct impact on the captain, causing him to go crazy and repeatedly say, 'She saved my life.' This relationship highlights the emotional toll of loss.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="THE CAPTAIN" target="THE PEOPLE AROUND HIM">
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
  <data key="d6">The captain's mental breakdown due to his wife's death had a lasting impact on the people around him, including those who witnessed his behavior and the emotional aftermath of the event.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="WHITESAND BAY" target="THE GROUP">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the group and the decision to visit Whitesand Bay is influenced by the girl's suggestion. This decision reflects the group's desire for relaxation and enjoyment, and it serves as a final act of leisure before returning home.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-1fb3571cbea81efe6dfda2aa2199fdbe</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="THE HONEST SAILOR" target="H.M.S. AGAMEMNON">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The sailor's experience aboard H.M.S. Agamemnon during the Crimean War influenced his perspective on life and the sea. This relationship connects his personal history with the broader historical context.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="THE HONEST SAILOR" target="THE TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The sailor's storytelling about the Crimean War and his experiences aboard H.M.S. Agamemnon created a connection with the travelers, offering them insights into maritime history and personal anecdotes.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="THE CRIMEAN WAR" target="THE TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The sailors' experience during the Crimean War indirectly influenced the travelers' understanding of the historical and cultural significance of the Land's End and surrounding areas.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="THE CAUTIOUS ONE" target="THE GROUP">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the cautious one and the group is one of influence and caution. Her concerns about the safety of the beach affect the group's decision to avoid it, highlighting the impact of her perspective on their actions.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-1fb3571cbea81efe6dfda2aa2199fdbe</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="FISHER WIFE" target="CHILDREN">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the fisher wife and her children is one of care and concern. She discusses her children's achievements and their education, showing her investment in their future. This relationship is central to the narrative and reflects the values of the local community, particularly the emphasis on education and family.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-a5e39286406a99236327034e4840f578</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="PRINCESS FREDERICA OF HANOVER" target="BARON VON PAWEL-RAMMINGEN">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the Baron Von Pawel-Rammingen and the Princess Frederica of Hanover is a marital and personal one. He is described as having loved, wooed, and married her, indicating a deep emotional and familial bond. This relationship is significant as it forms the basis of the royal visit and the public's interest in their story.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-3d113b2b3dce6fe310bc06ea08b364cb</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="PRINCESS FREDERICA OF HANOVER" target="PUBLIC">
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the public and the Princess Frederica of Hanover is one of curiosity and admiration. The public is interested in the princess's story and speculates about her arrival. This relationship is significant as it reflects the societal fascination with royalty and the impact of the royal visit on the local community.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-3d113b2b3dce6fe310bc06ea08b364cb</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="UTHER PENDRAGON" target="YGRAYNE">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">Uther Pendragon and Ygrayne had a romantic relationship that led to Uther's violent actions against her husband. Uther's desire for Ygrayne resulted in the siege of their castles, the killing of her husband, and their subsequent marriage. This relationship is central to the origin of Arthur's story and highlights themes of passion and betrayal.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="UTHER PENDRAGON" target="ARTHURIAN LEGENDS">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">Uther Pendragon and King Arthur have a direct father-son relationship, which is central to the Arthurian legends. Uther's actions and legacy shape the narrative, particularly in the context of the ideals of knighthood and chivalry that Arthur represents. This relationship underscores the themes of power, destiny, and moral responsibility.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-68ddd89fc902a4696967a79c5aea8055</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="YGRAYNE" target="KING UTHER PENDRAGON">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">Ygrayne is connected to King Uther Pendragon through the legend of her being shut up in a castle. This relationship is part of the Arthurian mythos and highlights the tragic and romantic elements of the narrative. The connection between Ygrayne and Uther Pendragon underscores the complex dynamics of power and love in the Arthurian legends.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MERLIN" target="ARTHUR">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">Merlin played a critical role in the upbringing of Arthur by arranging for him to be taken from the sea-shore beneath Tintagel and placed under the care of Sir Ector. This relationship established the foundation of Arthur's early life and influenced his path to becoming king. Merlin's guidance shaped Arthur's destiny and the magical elements of the legend.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GUINEVERE" target="ARTHUR">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">Arthur and Guinevere were married, but their relationship was strained when Guinevere fell in love with Sir Launcelot. This betrayal led to the disintegration of the Round Table and the downfall of Arthur's kingdom. The relationship between Arthur and Guinevere is a key element of the legend, symbolizing the fragility of love and loyalty.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GUINEVERE" target="SIR LAUNCELOT">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">Sir Launcelot and Guinevere had a romantic relationship that caused Guinevere to forsake Arthur. This relationship led to the weakening of Arthur's court and the eventual downfall of the Round Table. The bond between Sir Launcelot and Guinevere is a central theme in the legend, highlighting the consequences of forbidden love.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MORDRED" target="ARTHUR">
  <data key="d5">1.6</data>
  <data key="d6">Mordred and Arthur had a hostile relationship that culminated in Mordred's rebellion and Arthur's defeat at Camelford. Mordred's betrayal marked the end of Arthur's reign and the beginning of his mysterious disappearance. This relationship is a key element of the legend, representing the ultimate betrayal of a king by his own kin.&lt;SEP&gt;The Battle of Camelford was a direct consequence of Mordred's rebellion against Arthur. This relationship marked the end of Arthur's reign and the beginning of his mysterious disappearance. The battle is a key event in the legend, symbolizing the fall of a great king and the loss of his dream of a perfect kingdom.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="SIR BEDEVERE" target="ARTHUR">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">Arthur and Sir Bedevere shared a loyal relationship, with Sir Bedevere carrying out Arthur's final request to throw Excalibur into the lake. This act marked the end of Arthur's life and the beginning of his mysterious disappearance. The relationship between Arthur and Sir Bedevere is symbolic of loyalty and duty in the legend.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ORDER OF KNIGHTS OF THE ROUND TABLE" target="ARTHUR">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The Order of Knights of the Round Table was directly founded by Arthur, and its members were bound by a code of honor and service. This relationship established the structure of Arthur's court and the ideals of chivalry. The Round Table symbolized equality and unity among the knights, which was central to Arthur's vision of justice.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ARTHUR" target="LEGEND OF ARTHUR'S DISAPPEARANCE">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between Arthur and the legend of his disappearance is indirect, as it stems from the events of the Battle of Camelford and the throwing of Excalibur into the lake. This second-order relationship has influenced the cultural perception of Arthur as a mythical figure who may return to restore order. The legend of Arthur's disappearance has become a powerful symbol of hope and renewal.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ARTHUR" target="MORGANE LA FAYE">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">Arthur is closely associated with Morgane la Faye, who is believed to have held him in her lap at the time of his death. This relationship is rooted in the Arthurian legend and reflects the mystical and supernatural elements of the narrative. The bond between them is symbolic of the tragic end of Arthur's reign and the lingering presence of his legacy in Cornish folklore.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ARTHUR" target="TINTAGEL CASTLE">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">Tintagel Castle is directly linked to the legend of Arthur and Ygrayne. It is described as a place where Arthur and Ygrayne were connected, and it is part of the Arthurian mythos. The castle serves as a physical and symbolic representation of the Arthurian legends, reinforcing the cultural and historical significance of the site.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="TRISTRAM" target="ISEULT">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">Tristram is linked to Iseult through the legend of their love affair. This relationship is central to the Arthurian lore and highlights the themes of love, betrayal, and loss. The connection between Tristram and Iseult adds emotional depth to the narrative, emphasizing the tragic outcomes of their relationship.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ISEULT" target="KING MARK">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">Iseult and King Mark share a complex relationship rooted in the Arthurian legends. King Mark is Iseult's husband, but she is also involved in a forbidden love affair with Tristan. This relationship highlights the moral and emotional conflicts within the narrative, as well as the societal norms of the time.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-68ddd89fc902a4696967a79c5aea8055</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ISEULT" target="ARTHURIAN LEGENDS">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The Arthurian legends influence the broader cultural and historical context of the narrative, shaping the characters and events described. They provide a framework for understanding the moral and ethical dilemmas faced by the characters, as well as the ideals of knighthood and chivalry that are contrasted with the more brutal aspects of the story.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-68ddd89fc902a4696967a79c5aea8055</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CHOUGH" target="TWIN RUINS">
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the chough and the twin ruins is indirect, as both are part of the same landscape and cultural heritage. The chough's presence in the area is linked to the historical and mythical significance of the ruins, reinforcing the mystical atmosphere of the location. This relationship contributes to the overall sense of mystery and historical depth.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="DINAH MARIA CRAIK" target="JOURNEY">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between Dinah Maria Craik and the narrative of the journey is direct and personal. As the author, she provides the perspective through which the reader experiences the journey and the reflections on history and legend. Her voice is central to the interpretation of the landscape and the cultural significance of the places visited.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-9dca5f3a8de43d3e2bc3bc88b3703f62</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="SUBTERRANEAN CHAMBERS" target="HISTORY">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the subterranean chambers and the historical context is one of mystery and discovery. The chambers are described as ancient and enigmatic, with their purpose lost in time. This relationship is significant as it highlights the historical and archaeological interest in the region, adding a sense of intrigue to the narrative.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="DRIVER'S LOSS" target="DRIVER'S LIFESTYLE">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the driver's personal loss and his current lifestyle is one of resilience and adaptation. The driver has overcome the loss of his wife and child, maintaining a strong and active life without alcohol. This relationship is significant as it illustrates the driver's strength and ability to cope with adversity.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="STORM" target="APPRECIATION FOR LIFE">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The storm indirectly affects the mood and activities of the characters, leading to a greater appreciation for the simple pleasures of life. This third-order relationship highlights how external conditions can shape internal emotions and perspectives. The storm's impact extends beyond immediate challenges to influence long-term attitudes.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-9baf49d70e5cdf906cbc1334a05b60e0</data>
  <data key="d8">3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="STORM" target="SHIP">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the storm and the ship is a direct cause-and-effect relationship. The storm arose immediately after the pilot's blessing, leading to the ship's destruction. This relationship underscores the power of nature and the unpredictable forces that can affect human endeavors.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LAUNCE-FISHING" target="SAND-EEL">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">Launce-fishing is a local activity that involves catching sand-eels using a crooked iron spear. This relationship highlights the practical and recreational aspects of the activity, emphasizing its significance in the local culture and the experiences of the characters during their stay at the Lizard.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-0a11868bbedb00db5ad94f79da20acdd</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="THE GROUP" target="BOTALLACK MINE">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the group and the decision to avoid Botallack Mine is influenced by the girl's preference for leisure over exploration. This decision reflects the group's priorities and the impact of individual preferences on collective choices.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-1fb3571cbea81efe6dfda2aa2199fdbe</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LEGEND OF ARTHUR'S DISAPPEARANCE" target="CULTURAL IDENTITY OF ENGLAND">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the legend of Arthur and the cultural identity of England is indirect, as it stems from the belief that Arthur will return to restore the Order of Knights of the Round Table. This third-order relationship has shaped the national consciousness and inspired literary works such as Tennyson's poetry. The legend of Arthur continues to influence modern interpretations of heroism and leadership.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d8">3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="OLD MAN" target="DONKEYS">
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
  <data key="d6">The interaction between the old man and his donkeys at Bossinney Cove reflects a second-order relationship, as it indirectly connects to the broader themes of the narrative. The old man's daily routine and his care for his animals highlight the human element of the story, contrasting with the more mythical and historical aspects of the Arthurian legends.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-68ddd89fc902a4696967a79c5aea8055</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="PILOT" target="SHIP">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the pilot and the ship is direct and significant. The pilot was aboard the ship that carried the bells, and he was the one who gave the captain a blessing before the ship met its tragic end. This relationship highlights the human element in the story and the consequences of the pilot's words, which were interpreted as a sign of bad luck.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
</graph></graphml>